<?xml version='1.0' ?>
<rrx namespace="ValidationsAndTextAdjustments" v="9.1.9.7">
  <com ref="Datacap.Libraries.ValidationsAndTextAdjustments.Actions"  qi="Description of action library">
    <h>
      The Validations and Text Adjustments action library focus on actions that perform validation and manipulation
      of recognized text within an application.  When recognizing a field it is recommended to configure the field
      properties for the target engine to correctly specify the type of text being recognized and limit the
      field to the expected character set.  For example, if a field is expected to be numeric only, then the field
      should be preset to recognize only numeric values.  Perhaps a field may have some other restricted character set
      such as numeric values plus "A, B, C, D, E, F", then the field can be setup to allow just those characters.  This
      improves the recognition quality.<br/><br/>

      It isn't always possible to tell the recognition engine exactly what is expected.  This action library can help
      filter out invalid characters or flag fields that do not match expected patterns or expected data types.  For example,
      if a date does not match the expected date pattern for the locale, then the validation actions can flag the field
      so it can be examined by a verification operator.  Likewise, a validation check can be done for numeric formats or totals
      of several numeric fields.<br/><br/>

      Upon failure of a tested condition, most of the actions in this action library will also store the
      reason for the message in a standard variable called Message.  This DCO variable is used by client
      verification panels to display messages to verify operators so they can understand why a page
      has failed a verification ruleset. If there are multiple reasons for a failure, these messages are only
      displayed to the user one at a time, so the operator must fix the displayed error, rerun the validation
      test and see if additional verification errors still remain.  If errors still remain, the operator will
      need to repeat the process until they have rectified all of the issues that are causing a page
      to fail validation.  Typically a the rule for a validation action is attached to the
      DCO object that needs to pass the validation so the message is associated with the failed object
      and typically the status of the object will be set to "1" to indicate the failure in a verification panel.<br/><br/>

      For example, one common validation is that a particular field is required to have a particular length. Perhaps
      it has a minimum length, or a maximum length, or both.  The validation action that checks a field's
      length can be added to a validation ruleset to confirm that the length is correct.  If the length is incorrect
      after a recognition step, it can flag the failed field to the operator in the verify panel. Likewise, it
      can revalidate a field after it has been reviewed by an operator to help confirm the field is now at
      the required length before moving on to the next page. The more validations that can be placed on the
      field data for an application will provide a greater assurance that the data on the page was accurately
      recognized or correctly entered by an operator.<br/><br/>

      <b>Locale Sensitive Operations</b><br/><br/>

      The validation actions are sensitive to the locale where it is applicable.  For example, date validation and currency
      validation are performed based on the current locale.  If not set within the application, the locale used is the
      machine's default Windows locale.  The locale for the entire application can be set within the Application Manager,
      which will override the windows locale, and instead use the locale configured for the entire application.<br/><br/>

      Datacap clients and actions support multiple locales within a single application and multiple locales within a single
      batch. The hierarchical DCO variable hr_locale, can be specified to control the locale of the DCO object being
      processed.<br/><br/>

      rrSet("en-GB", "@X.hr_locale)<br/>
      This example sets the locale for the current DCO node and it's children objects to English (United Kingdom).
      rrSet can be used to set the value at runtime, or the value for hr_locale can be set in the setup DCO, if needed.
      When specifying locales, use standard LCIDs, and these are case sensitive.<br/><br/>

      If set at the batch level, it affects the entire application.  If set on a document DCO node, then it sets the
      locale for all pages and fields within that document.  This can be controlled at any level. If set at the page level
      then the locale will be set for the entire page and its fields, allowing each page within a document to have a unique locale.
      For example, one page could have a US locale with US formatted data while the next page could have a French locale with French formatted data.
      It is possible, although not usually required, to have unique locales configured for each field within
      a single page.<br/><br/><br/>

      <b>Smart Parameters - Brief Guide</b><br/><br/>

      The top level help of the RuleRunnerLogic action library contains the complete smart parameter reference.
      Here is a short set of examples.<br/><br/>
      
      If an action supports smart parameters, it is specified in the action help.  Smart parameters are very powerful
      specifications for parameters that allow access of data to and from other DCO objects and perform
      special pre-defined operations in one step.<br/><br/>

      Smart parameters can be used to dynamically obtain or create action input parameters at runtime.  For example,
      an action could be fed the value of a field after recognition, a parameter could pull the value from a
      DCO variable that was set by a previous action, or a smart parameter could evaluate to the current date, the current
      time, the current job ID, the current operator ID, the path to the current batch that is running, and more.
      Smart parameter syntax can also be used to build expressions at runtime that concatenate dynamic data and
      static data at runtime.  Smart parameters are
      a flexible way to provide a wide range of input to any action that supports smart parameters.<br/><br/>
      
      The following are some examples of manipulation that are common for manipulating data where it may be expected
      that a special action exists in the this action library but is not required.  This summary is intended to
      show some manipulation that is typical for validation tasks.  This set of examples are by no means
      the limit of how smart parameters can be used.  While many of these examples show the
      value being passed to the handy action rrSet, the same smart parameter can be passed to any action
      that supports smart parameters as input.<br/><br/>
      
      Refer to the full smart parameter guide in the top level help of the RuleRunnerLogic actions
      for full details on all available smart parameters and how to use them.<br/><br/>

      Append Text To A Field:<br/>
      rrSet("Append This +@F","@F")<br/>
      Assuming this is running on a field object, it appends the text "Append This " to the current text value of the field.
      If the field originally contained "ABCD", the final value is "Append This ABCD")<br/><br/>
      
      rrSet("Append This +@F+ The End","@F")<br/>
      Similarly, if the original field value is "ABCD", the final value of the field text is "Append This ABCD The End".<br/><br/>

      Concatenate Text From A Different Field:<br/>
      rrSet("@P\AnotherField+@F","@F")<br/>
      This example will grab the text value from the sibling field "AnotherField" and append the text to the front of the
      text in the current DCO field.<br/><br/>

      Concatenate Text From A Different Field Variable:<br/>
      rrSet("@P\AnotherField.MyVariable+@F","@F")<br/>
      This example will grab the value from the sibling field "AnotherField" variable called "MyVariable" and append the text to the front of the
      text in the current DCO field.<br/><br/>

      Concatenate Text From A Different Field Variable Placed Into New Variable:<br/>
      rrSet("@P\AnotherField.MyVariable+@CHR(32)+@F","@F.NewVariable")<br/>
      This example will grab the value from the sibling field "AnotherField" variable called "MyVariable" and append the text to the front of the
      text in the current DCO field. The current value of the field object is not updated, instead the new result is placed into a variable
      for the current field called "NewVariable".
      Assuming that "MyVariable" contains the text "Hello" and the current field value is "Tom", the first parameter will
      fully resolve to "Hello Tom" and pass that value to the rrSet action, which will then place that new string
      into the variable called "NewVariable".<br/><br/>

      Assigning A Default Field Value:<br/>
      rrSet("My Default Text","@P\MyField")<br/>
      Assuming this action is run on a page object that contains a field called "MyField", it initialized the default value of
      the field the "My Default Text".  If the field was already recognized, then this new text overwrites the existing text.
      If the field is later recognized, then this default text will be replaced by the recognized text.<br/><br/>

      Assigning Today's Date To A Field:<br/>
      rrSet("@DATE(dd.mm.yyyy)","@X")<br/>
      This example shows how to assign the current date in day, month, year format when rules are run on
      a field object.  If a date format is not specified, the @DATE smart parameter will default to MM/DD/YYYY.<br/><br/>

      Likewise, the smart parameter @TIME() can be used to put a time stamp into a field or as a parameter to an action
      that supports smart parameters.<br/><br/>

      Assigning The Current Job ID A Field:<br/>
      rrSet("@JOBID","@X")<br/>
      Assuming this is called on a field level object, this example shows how to assign the current job ID to the text value of a
      field object.<br/><br/>

      Is the current Job ID the expected Job ID:<br/>
      rrCompare("@JOBID","MyJobID")<br/>
      This compares the current Job ID with the string "MyJobID" and returns true if it matches.  If needed
      the hard coded "MyJobID" could reference a smart parameter that contains the value. Alternatively,
      rrCompareNot can be used to return true if the current Job ID is does not match the provided string.
      Other comparison actions perform case insensitive comparisons.<br/><br/>

      Clear a field's value:<br/>
      rrSet("@EMPTY","@F\MyField")<br/>
      This example shows how to empty the recognized value of the field "MyField" using a rule called on a page that
      contains the "MyField" child.<br/><br/>

      To continue if a field is empty:<br/>
      rrCompare("@EMPTY","@X)<br/>
      This action would be called on the field object.
      The smart parameters can reference hard coded values or obtain
      values from other objects or other DCO variables.<br/><br/>

      To continue if a field is empty from page level:<br/>
      rrCompare("@EMPTY","@X\MyField")<br/>
      This tests to see if the page's field called "MyField" is empty. If so, the rule continues.<br/><br/>

      To continue if a field is not empty:<br/>
      rrCompareNot("@EMPTY","@X")<br/>
      This action would be called on the field object to check the text value of the field.
      If the current field is empty, the function will continue to the next action
      in the rule.  If the field contains
      text, then control will branch to the next function in the ruleset.<br/><br/>

      To test if a field value is greater than a specific number:<br/>
      rrCompareNumeric("1234", "˂", "@X")<br/>
      This action tests to see if the numeric value of the current field text is
      greater than 1234.  Parameters can be changed as needed.  For example, if "@X" was
      replaced with "@D.MyVar", the value 1234 will be compared against the
      value of the parent document level variable called "MyVar".<br/><br/>

      Test if a field is hidden:<br/>
      rrCompare("-1","@X.STATUS")<br/>
      If a field has a STATUS of "-1", it is considered to be a hidden field or ignored and is not displayed
      in a client verification panel.  If rules logic needs to determine if a field is hidden,
      it can be tested by checking the STATUS variable of the field to see if it is "-1".
      This example assumes the action is running on the current field object whose status will
      be checked.  Of course, smart parameters can be used to navigate and check the status
      of other DCO objects.<br/><br/>
    </h>

      <method name="AddAllLineitemTaxesToTaxField" qi="Adds all tax values from detail structure into Tax field.">
          <ap>
              None<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              Adds all tax values from detail structure into Tax field.<br/><br/>

              This action is not for general use. It is intended for an application, like APT,
              where the application has a tax detail field that contains a field lineitems of taxes
              called "TaxLineitem".
              The application must also have another field called "Tax", which will be updated
              with the result of this action. <br/><br/>

              This action will set the position of the tax field to "0,0,0,0".<br/><br/>
              
              <e>
                  <b>AddAllLineitemTaxesToTaxField()</b>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              The close event of TaxLineitem detail field in APT.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>True.</b> <br/>
          </ret>
      </method>
  
      
    <method name="AddPaddingCharacterToStart" qi="Extends a field or variable to a specified length using a specific padding character.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="PadCharacter" type="string" />
      <p name="MaximumLength" type="string" />
      <ap>
        Target : A smart parameter that resolves to the target DCO object or variable to pad with characters to the start of the string.<br/>
        PadCharacter : The character to pad in front of the text to extend it to the maximum length.<br/>
        MaximumLength : The target path and filename.<br/><br/>
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Accepts a specification for a DCO field or DCO variable and extends the length of the text by placing
        the specified character in front of the existing text until the length is at the specified maximum.<br/><br/>
        
        If the current text length is at, or beyond, the specified maximum length then the existing text
        will not be changed.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>AddPaddingCharacterToStart("@F", "0", "10")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a feild object that contains the recognized value "2240.00", 
          it will be adjusted to "0002240.00"<br/><br/>

          <b>AddPaddingCharacterToStart("@P\Total", " ", "10")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a page object that has a child field called "Total",
          this action will pad the recognized value of "Total" with leading spaces until
          the length reaches 10 characters. If the field "Total" has a text
          value of "Hello", it will add 5 spaces to the start of the text in
          the field.<br/><br/>

          <b>AddPaddingCharacterToStart("@X.MyVariable", "@STRING("@")", "15")</b><br/>
          This action will pad the text stored in the object's variable called "MyVariable".
          The text will pad the beginning of the text with an @ symbol until it is 
          15 characters in length.  Because the @ symbol is a special smart parameter
          it must be specified using the @STRING or @CHR smart parameter notation
          to prevent it from being interpreted as a smart parameter.
          If the variable "MyVariable" has the text "Jack and Jill", the final value
          of "MyVariable" will be "@@Jack and Jill".<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any Level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if one of the parameters is invalid or if the target string specified is invalid; otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        The top level help topic for general information about this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="AddPaddingCharacterToEnd" qi="Extends a field or variable to a specified length using a specific padding character.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="PadCharacter" type="string" />
      <p name="MaximumLength" type="string" />
      <ap>
        Target : A smart parameter that resolves to the target DCO object or variable to pad with a character.<br/>
        PadCharacter : The character to pad in front of the text to extend it to the maximum length.<br/>
        MaximumLength : The target path and filename.  Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Accepts a specification for a DCO field or variable and extends the length of the text by placing
        the specified character at the end of the existing text until the length is at the specified maximum.<br/><br/>

        If the current text length is at, or beyond, the specified maximum length then the existing text
        will not be changed.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>AddPaddingCharacterToEnd("@F", "0", "10")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a feild object that contains the recognized value "2240.00",
          it will be adjusted to "2240.00000"<br/><br/>

          <b>AddPaddingCharacterToEnd("@P\Total", " ", "10")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a page object that has a child field called "Total",
          this action will pad the recognized value of "Total" with trailing spaces until
          the length reaches 10 characters. If the field "Total" has a text
          value of "Hello", it will add 5 spaces to the end of the text in
          the field.<br/><br/>

          <b>AddPaddingCharacterToEnd("@X.MyVariable", "@STRING("@")", "15")</b><br/>
          This action will pad the text stored in the object's variable called "MyVariable".
          The text will pad the end of the text with an @ symbol until it is
          15 characters in length.  Because the @ symbol is a special smart parameter
          it must be specified using the @STRING or @CHR smart parameter notation
          to prevent it from being interpreted as a smart parameter.
          If the variable "MyVariable" has the text "Jack and Jill", the final value
          of "MyVariable" will be "Jack and Jill@@".<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any Level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if one of the parameters is invalid or if the target string specified is invalid; otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        The top level help topic for general information about this action library.
      </see>
    </method>


      <method name="AddToErrorMessage">
          <p name="p1" type="string" qi="Error message text."/>
          <ap>
              Smart parameters are supported.
          </ap>
          <h>              
              This action allows for custom messages to be displayed to the verify operator when a validation fails.
              The provided message is appended to any existing error message.
              How the message is displayed back to user depends on the verify client used.
              <e>
                  <b>AddToErrorMsg("Vendor Number cannot be blank.")</b>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>False.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>
              ClearErrorMessage
          </see>
      </method>

      <method name="AddToLineItemDetailErrorMessage">
          <p name="p1" type="string" qi="Error message text."/>
          <ap>
              Smart parameters are supported.
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action allows for custom messages to be displayed to the verify operator when a validation fails for line items.
              The provided message is appended to any existing error message.
              How the message is displayed back to user depends on the verify client used.
              <e>
                  <b>AddToLineItemDetailErrorMessage("Description cannot be blank.")</b>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Field level. Specifically, it must be a line item detail field.</lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>False.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>
              ClearErrorMessage
          </see>
      </method>
    
    <method name="AllowOnlyCharacters" qi="Filters out characters not contained in the allowed set.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="AllowedCharacters" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to be updated.<br/>
        <b>AllowedCharacters</b> A value that specifies permitted characters in the current word. 
        An empty parameter removes all characters. Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action removes all characters NOT in the parameter from the a Field's recognized value
        or from a DCO variable.  If the target is a field's recognized text, the confidence of the
        remaining characters will not be changed by this operation.<br/><br/>

        An empty argument will remove ALL characters. The specified characters are case sensitive so
        if characters should be removed regardless of case, add the upper and lower case characters to be removed.<br/><br/>
        <e>
            <b>AllowOnlyCharacters("@F", "ABCDEFG.")</b><br/>
            Calling this action on a field DCO object, if the field value is "HELLO DOLLY." it becomes "ED."
            The order of the characters in the input parameter have no affect on the result.<br/><br/>

            <b>AllowOnlyCharacters("@X.MyVariable", "ABCDEFG.")</b><br/>
            The action AllowOnlyCharacters" will filter the text within the DCO variable "MyVariable"
            on the current DCO object.<br/><br/>

            <b>AllowOnlyCharacters("@F", "AaBbCc+@STRING(@+)")</b><br/>
            There are times when characters need to be passed to an action that are also special smart parameter symbols.
            Because the characters + and @ are special smart parameter symbols, they must be specified
            using syntax which allows them to be added without interpretation.  
            The @STRING specification allows the text within it to be passed to the action without smart parameter processing.
            The final result from
            the example is that the characters "AaBcCc@+" will be the only characters allowed in the final value.<br/><br/>

            <b>AllowOnlyCharacters("@F", "@STRING(AaBbCc@+)")</b><br/>
            Smart parameters are processed as tokens that can often be specified multiple ways,
            producing the same final result.  This example is an alternate specification of the above
            example.  Instead of separating parts of the character input, because the entire input
            string is static, the entire input string can also be specified as a single value within
            the @STRING token.  This example produces the same resolved input parameter of "AaBcCc@+".
            <br/><br/>

            <b>AllowOnlyCharacters("@F", "@P\Field1+@STRING(AaBbCc@+)")</b><br/>
            Sometimes input to an action needs to come from another location, or data that is captured
            at runtime. This example shows another use of smart parameters to combine dynamic text from a field
            and hard coded text. The example would be called on a field object who has a sibling
            field called "Field1". Assuming the text value of Field1 is "XYZ", the final parameter
            passed to the action evaluates to "XYZAaBbCc@+".
            <br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False,</b> if called on the wrong level.  Otherwise <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
          The top level help topic for general information about this action library.<br/><br/>
          The top level help of the RuleRunnerLogic action library contains the complete smart parameter reference.
      </see>
    </method>


      <method name="AlterDateByDay" qi="Adds or subtracts days from a date text.">
          <p name="target" type="string" qi ="A smart parameter that specifies the target field or variable to update."/>
          <p name="days" type="string" qi="The number of days to add to the date."/>
          <ap>
              Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              The action will add or subtract the specified number of days to the date text. The target text to update can be in a field
              or a variable.<br/><br/>
              
              The action requires that the date format match that of the current locale.  The hr_locale variable is respected, if set.
              The action will output the
              updated date in the default format for the current locale. The output date may or may not have leading zeros
              for single digit dates.<br/><br/>
              
              The original character confidence is not changed.<br/>
              <e>
                  rrSet("04/01/2025", "@F")<br/>
                  <b>AlterDatebyDay("@F", "7")</b><br/><br/>

                  This example adds one week to the date contained in the field.  If the date crosses
                  over a month or year boundary, they will be adjusted appropriately.
                  The value 04/01/2025 is changed to 4/8/2025.
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              Always, <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>
      

    <method name="CalculateDateDifference" qi="Calculates a date difference in days, months, quarters or years.">
      <p name="StartDate" type="string" />
      <p name="EndDate" type="string" />
      <p name="TargetVariable" type="string" />
      <p name="DateProperty" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>startDate</b> : The starting date.<br/>
        <b>endDate</b> : The ending date.<br/>
        <b>targetVariable</b> : The variable to hold the calculated result value.<br/>
        <b>dateProperty</b> : The value to calculate.  0 = days, 1 = months, 2 = quarters, 3 = years.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Calculates the number of days, months, quarters or years between two dates.
        Only whole numbers are returned.  Any fractional parts of the value are dropped.
        Quarters are calculated simply by dividing the number of months by 3.
        The order of the dates does not matter.<br/><br/>

        Note this action only supports Gregorian short dates as input and the date format must match the 
        default format of the current locale.<br/><br/>

        If the date requirements are met, the action returns true.  If the date requirements are not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action, so it is typically useful to call this action on the DCO object
        that is being checked so the message is associated with the same object.   Typically this would be
        called on a field object which needs to pass this validation test.<br/><br/>
        
        <e>
          <b>CalculateDateDifference("4/20/2012", "5/19/2012", "@P.Months", 1)</b><br/>
          This example will create the page variable "Months" with a value of "0".<br/><br/>

          <b>CalculateDateDifference("4/20/2012", "5/20/2012", "@P.Months", 1)</b><br/>
          This example will create the page variable "Months" with a value of "1".<br/><br/>

          <b>CalculateDateDifference("4/20/2012", "4/19/2013", "@P.Years", 3)</b><br/>
          This example will create the page variable "Years" with a value of "0".<br/><br/>

          <b>CalculateDateDifference("4/20/2012", "4/20/2013", "@P.Years", 3)</b><br/>
          This example will create the page variable "Years" with a value of "1".<br/><br/>

          <b>CalculateDateDifference(@P\MyDate1, @P\MyDate2, "@P.Days", 0)</b><br/>
          This example will create the page variable "Days" with the number of days between the dates specified by the values in field MyDate1 and field MyDate2.<br/><br/>

          <b>CalculateDateDifference(@P.MyDate1, @P.MyDate2, "@P.Days", 0)</b><br/>
          This example will create the page variable "Days" with the number of days between the dates specified by page variables MyDate1 and MyDate2.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False,</b> If the format of either date is invalid.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters and the use of the current locale.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>
    

    <method name="CalculateFields" qi="Performs numeric calculations on fields and evaluates the result.">
      <p name="Equation" type="string" />
      <p name="DecimalDigits" type="string" />
      <p name="PreserveStatus" type="string" />
      <ap>        
        <b>Equation:</b> The equation that is the basis for the calculation.<br/>
        You can use a Field object's name or numeric values with any arithmetic operator (+,-,*,/,^).
        To use a Field object's name, or numeric value, surround it with single quotation marks (').
        An empty value is treated as a "0".<br/><br/>
        
        <b>DecimalDigits:</b> The number of decimal places to limit the logical comparison. If left blank, or invalid, the
        number will default to the value for the current locale.<br/><br/>

        <b>PreserveStatus:</b> Can be "True" or "False" to indicate if he status of all associated fields if the calculation fails should
        be updated based on success or failure of the operation. If not provided, or invalid, the value will default to False.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Calculates the equation entered as a parameter.  The equation can contain field names which will
        use the field's value at runtime.  The equation can contain mathematical operations +, -, *, and /.
        A single equality can also be performed such as =, ˂, =˂, =>, >, and ˂>.<br/><br/>

        If the equation contains a boolean equality, the action will determine if the equation evaluates to true or false.
        If the result is false, then status of the fields specified within the equation
        will be set to "1", allowing them to be reviewed by a following task
        such as a verification operator.  The equation should be specified so that two sides of
        the equation can be evaluated to determine if the entire equation is correct.<br/><br/>

        <b>Note:</b> If the result does not match the equation's result, dependent fields (fields involved in the
        equation) will receive a Failed status and will appear pink in the applicable field of the Client's Verification panel.<br/><br/>

        If the results of a previously failed CalculateFields call are corrected by a user, such that the calculation no longer returns false, dependent fields that
        were set to status Failed by the CalculateFields action will be updated to status Pass.<br/><br/>

        It is likely that dependent fields could have their statuses updated via other rules and actions, 
        so it is important when configuring your application to properly stage,
        and sequence your validations action logic to work in tandem with the CalculateFields action.<br/><br/>

        It is important to be aware that smart parameters are supported for all parameters.  Smart parameters use special
        characters such as "+".  To use a "+" in an equation, it is necessary to specify the text so it is not
        interpreted as a special character.  This can be achieved using the @STRING or @CHR smart parameters
        as shown in the example.<br/><br/>

        If the equality calculation equation is successful, the action returns true.  If the equation is not satisfied, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action, so it is typically useful to call this action on the DCO object
        that is being checked so the message is associated with the same object.   Typically this would be
        called on a field object which needs to pass this validation test.<br/><br/>
        
        If the specified equation does not contain an equality operation, then the resulting
        value will be calculated and stored in the text value of the current field object.
        If called on a page level object, then the calculated value will be stored in
        a DCO variable called "CalculateField" and the action will return true.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>CalculateFields("@STRING('SubTotal' + 'Shipping' + 'Tax' = 'Total')", "2", "False")</b><br/>
          This example sums the "SubTotal", "Shipping" and "Tax" fields and checks to see if the total
          of the three fields equals the value in the "Total" field.  If they are not the same,
          then the status will be changed to "1" for each of the fields.<br/><br/>
          
          The above example places the entire expression into the "@STRING" smart parameter keyword to
          prevent any pre-processing of the expression, particularly the + signs within the expression, which
          would otherwise be first interpretated as smart parameter operators, changing the evaluated expression.<br/><br/>

          <b>CalculateFields("('SubTotal' +@CHR(43)+ 'Shipping' +@CHR(43)+ 'Tax') - '0.05' = 'Total'", "2", "False")</b><br/>
          This example is similar to the above example, but also includes a hard coded value
          within the equation. The smart parameter @CHR(43) evaluates to a plus sign.  Because the parameter is
          supports smart parameters, the + will be interpreted as a smart parameter and change the input string.
          This technique will resolve to a + in the final equation like this:<br/>
          ('SubTotal' + 'Shipping' + 'Tax') - '0.05' = 'Total'<br/><br/>

          The above example takes an alternate approach by using @CHR(43) to insert the + sign into the expression.<br/><br/>

          To check for a 'tolerance' use two actions in sequence like this:<br/>
          <b>CalculateFields("@STRING(('Wages' + 'Interest' + 'Unemployment')&gt;=('Gross'- '.05'))", "2", "False")</b><br/>
          <b>CalculateFields("@STRING(('Wages' + 'Interest' + 'Unemployment')&lt;=('Gross'+ '.05'))", "2", "False")</b><br/>
          The example will calculate fields twice and allow the range of values.  If both tests pass, then they will both return
          True.  If either test fails, the action will return false and set the status of the field to "1" indicating there is
          a problem with the field.<br/><br/>

          <b>CalculateFields("'SubTotal' +@CHR(43)+ 'Shipping' +@CHR(43)+ 'Tax')", "0", "False")</b><br/>
          This example will sum the values of the "SubTotal", "Shipping" and "Tax" fields and stores the result
          in the text value of the current field, or if called on a page object, the value will be stored
          in a variable called "CalculateFields". This calculated value can be accessed by subsequent actions.<br/><br/>

          <b>CalculateFields("'SubTotal' * '2')", "0", "False")</b><br/>
          This example finds the value of the SubTotal field and multiplies it by 2 and stores the result.<br/><br/>

          Some numeric operators used for field calculations are also used as smart parameter tokens. When a parameter
          allows smart parameters, the parameter will always be processed for smart parameter tokens first. The
          @STRING() token is a common way to pass these values without interpretation.  If an action is not processing
          correctly, it is likely due to a numeric symbol being processed as a smart parameter.  Check the action log
          to see if the evaluation statement is built as expected and adjust the action parameters as required.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page or Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the expression evaluates to True. <br/>
        <b>False</b> if the expression evaluates to False or the expression is not valid (eg. a field's value is not numeric).
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding the use of the current locale.<br/>
        The top level help of the RuleRunnerLogic action library contains the complete smart parameter reference.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


      <method name="CalculateInvoiceTotal" qi="Determines if the Total field is within the tolerance for the data on the page.">
          <p name="Tolerance"/>
          <ap>
              The floating point tolerance value that will be used to determine if the Tax, Shipping and Detail values equal the Total within the tolerance.<br/><br/>              
          </ap>
          <h>
              Uses the Details, Tax and Shipping fields to calculate the total value and checks to see if it matches the Total field within
              the specified tolerance.<br/><br/>

              This action is not for general use. It is intended for an application, like APT,
              where the application has a "Tax", "Shipping" and "Details" field.
              <e>
                  IsFieldCurrency()<br/>
                  <b>CalculateInvoiceTotal("0.02")</b><br/><br/>

                  In this example, the combined total of the Tax, Shipping and Details fields must be within .02 of the total field.<br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Must be called on the invoice total field.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>True</b> if the Details, Tax and Shipping fields add up to the Total field value within the specified tolerance value.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>

      <method name="CalculateLineItemQuantityAndPrice" qi="Determines if the LineTotal field is correct.">
          <p name="Tolerance"/>
          <ap>
              The floating point tolerance value that will be used to determine if the LineTotal matches the Qty * Price within the tolerance.<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              Determines if the fields "Qty * Price = LineTotal" within
              the specified tolerance value.<br/><br/>

              This action is not for general use. It is intended for an application, like APT,
              where the application has a lineitems field with a qty and price.<br/><br/>
              <e>
                  IsFieldCurrency()<br/>
                  <b>CalculateLineItemQuantityAndPrice("0.02")</b><br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Must be called on the Invoice_Total field.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>False</b> if the Qty, Price or LineTotal fields do not contain a numeric value.<br/>
              <b>True</b> if the Qty * Price matches the LineTotal value within the specified tolerance.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>

      <method name="ChangeTextToMixedCase" qi="Changes the text so the first letter of every word is upper-case.">
          <p name="Target" type="string" qi="A smart parameter that specifies a variable or field." />
          <ap>
              Smart parameters are supported.
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action changes text to be Title case where the first letter of
              each word will be capitalized.<br/><br/>
              <e>
                  <b>ChangeTextToMixedCase("@P\Field1")</b><br/><br/>

                  If the field in this example is "hello, i MUST be going.", the text will be changed to "Hello, I Must Be Going."
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>
              Refer to the top level help of the RuleRunnerLogic action library for the smart parameters reference guide.<br/>
          </see>
      </method>


      <method name="CheckAndSetCurrencyDecimalToPeriod" qi="Changes currency values to have a period decimal separator.">
          <p name="target" type="string" qi="A smart parameter that references a field or variable."/>
          <ap>
              The target location identifies the location of the text to check and update.
              <br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              The action updates the decimal separator on text that is expected to be a currency value, meaning
              that the value has two decimal places, such as "xxx.xx". The action will check the location where
              the decimal separator is expected.  If the character is a space, or a comma, the character
              will be replaced with a period. This action is not locale aware and will operate the same
              regardless of the currently selected locale.<br/><br/>
              
              This action can be used to fix errors where the decimal is not recognized and leaves a blank in that area,
              also this can be used for conversion for European numbers that use a comma to separate the whole amount from decimal
              portion. For example: "$100,00" becomes "$100.00" and "$100 00" becomes "$100.00".<br/><br/>
              <e>
                  CheckAndSetCurrencyDecimalToPeriod("@F")<br/>                  
                  AllowOnlyCharacters("0123456789.-")<br/>
                  IsFieldCurrency()<br/><br/>
                  
                  This example assumes it is called on a field object.
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              False, if an error occurs.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>
      
      
    <method name="CheckSubFields" qi="Checks values in a lineitem.">
      <p name="Expression" type="string" />
      <ap>
        An "expression" that specifies which LINEITEM child fields are to be checked for the presence or absence
        of captured values. Within the expression, each child Field object's name needs to be surrounded
        with single quotation marks ('). You can also use parentheses () in your expression. Action is placed on the
        parent of the LINEITEM field. Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>        
        In an application that uses lineitems, this action validates an instance of a 
        parent Field object by confirming the presence (or absence) of
        captured values for one or more of its child fields. (In the APT application, as an example,
        child fields of the LINEITEM parent include: ItemID, ItemDesc, Quantity, Price and LineTotal.)        
        <br/><br/>
        
        Invalid parent Field objects are deleted from the DCO if they do not meet this criteria.<br/><br/>
        This action should be configured to run in its own RuleSet (for example, in a Filter RuleSet) 
        because it can delete the DCO objects as rules are running and would be applied
        to the DETAILS Field object (parent object to the LINEITEM field) in an application that uses LINEITEMs such as APT.
        <e>
          <b>Example 1:</b> The captured values for a LINEITEM field are:<br/>
          ItemID = 12345<br/>
          ItemDesc =<br/>
          Price = 12.00<br/>
          LineTotal = <br/><br/>

          The action's parameter contains this expression:<br/>
          <b>CheckSubFields("('ItemID' OR 'ItemDesc') AND ('Price' OR 'LineTotal')")</b><br/><br/>

          In this example the action returns <b>True</b> and the current 	LINEITEM object is Valid.<br/><br/>

          <b>Example 2:</b> The captured values for the LINEITEM field are:<br/>
          ItemID = 12345<br/>
          ItemDesc = Thank you for your order<br/>
          Price =<br/>
          LineTotal = <br/><br/>

          The action's parameter contains this expression:<br/>
          <b>CheckSubFields("('ItemID' OR 'ItemDesc') AND ('Price' OR 'LineTotal')")</b><br/><br/>

          In this example the action's equation returns is <b>False</b> and the current LINEITEM
          object is Invalid. The field and the values of its child fields are deleted from the Data file.<br/><br/>

          <b>Example 3:</b>  The captured values for the LINEITEM field are:<br/>
          ItemID = 12345<br/>
          LineTotal = gonetolunch<br/><br/>

          The action's parameter contains this expression:<br/>
          <b>CheckSubFields("('ItemID') AND ('LineTotal')")</b><br/><br/>

          In this case, the action returns <b>True</b> and validates the LINEITEM field - despite the
          presence of a nonsense entry in the LineTotal child field!
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level. The parent of the LINEITEM field.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


      <method name="ClearErrorMessage" qi="Removes any previous error messages.">
          <ap>None.</ap>
          <h>
              This action clears the current error message. Notice that the paradigm is that the error message is cleared at the start
              of the function. After clearing the message, then the work will be performed, such as a validation. If the validation
              passes, then the message would remain empty. If an error occurs, then the message would be set with the new message.<br/>
              <e>
                  Status_Preserve_OFF()<br/>
                  <b>ClearErrorMsg()</b><br/>
                  CaptureOpInfo()<br/>
                  rrCompare("@P\Routing_Instructions","None")
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>



      <method name="ConvertFieldToCurrency" qi="Formats a numeric field as currency without a currency symbol.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Formats the current field's text value as a currency value without a currency symbol.  
        If the value does not have a decimal separator, the decimal separator will be added.
        The following steps are performed on the field:<br/><br/>
        
        1. Removes existing currency symbols.<br/>
        2. Replaces negative value characters such as parenthesis, 'NEG', 'CR' and trailing hyphen
        with a leading hyphen.<br/>
        3. If a decimal exists, its position is not changed.<br/>
        4. If a decimal does not exist and the field contains 2 or more characters, a decimal is inserted
        before the last two characters in the field.<br/>
        5. If a decimal does not exist and the field contains less than 2 characters, no decimal is inserted.<br/><br/>

        This action works on the recognized text value of a field.
        To convert a numeric value that is in a DCO variable, first use rrSet to copy the value into a field, then
        this action can be run on the field. Once complete, the value could be copied back to the original
        DCO variable.
        <e>
          <b>ConvertFieldToCurrency()</b><br/>
          A value of <b>1</b> remains <b>1</b><br/>
          A value of <b>12</b> becomes <b>.12</b><br/>
          A value of <b>105</b> becomes <b>1.05</b><br/>
          A value of <b>104.009</b> remains <b>104.009</b><br/>
          A value of <b>$104.009</b> becomes <b>104.009</b><br/>
          A value of <b>$12345</b> becomes <b>123.45</b><br/>
          A value of <b>($123.45)</b> becomes <b>-123.45</b><br/>
          A value of <b>$123.45 NEG</b> becomes <b>-123.45</b><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the text value is numeric and greater than one character. Otherwise, <b>False</b>.
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help item in this action library for more information about the use of the current locale.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="ConvertToLowerCase" qi="Converts text in a field or variable to lower case.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to be updated.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Converts any upper case characters in a field's recognized text value, or in a DCO variable, to lower case characters.
        If the target is a field's recognized text, the confidence of the characters will not be changed by this operation.<br/><br/>
        
        <e>
          <b>ConvertToLowerCase("@X")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field object, it will update the field's text value so it is all lower case.<br/><br/>

          <b>ConvertToLowerCase("@P\MyField")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a page object, it will update the text value for the DCO child field "MyField" 
          so it is all lower case.<br/><br/>

          <b>ConvertToLowerCase("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This example will update the DCO variable called "MyVariable" on the current DCO node so it is
          all lower case.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        ConvertToUpperCase<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="ConvertToUpperCase" qi="Converts text in a field or variable to upper case.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to be updated.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Converts any lower case characters in a field's recognized text value, or a DCO variable, to upper case characters.
        If the target is a field's recognized text, the confidence of the characters will not be changed by this operation.<br/><br/>
        
        <e>
          <b>ConvertToUpperCase("@X")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field, it will update the field's text value so it is all upper case.<br/><br/>

          <b>ConvertToUpperCase("@P\MyField")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a page, it will update the text value for the DCO child field "MyField"
          so it is all upper case.<br/><br/>

          <b>ConvertToUpperCase("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This example will update the DCO variable called "MyVariable" on the current DCO node so it is
          all upper case.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        ConvertToUpperCase<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="CreateGUID" qi="Creates a globally unique identifier">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to be updated.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Generates a new GUID value string and stores it in the target location.<br/><br/>

        <e>
          <b>CreateGUID("@X")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field, it will update the field's text value with a new GUID.<br/><br/>

          <b>CreateGUID("@P\MyField")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a page, it will update the text value for the DCO child field "MyField"
          with a new GUID.<br/><br/>

          <b>CreateGUID("@B.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This example will create a batch level variable called "MyVariable" and set it with a new GUID value.
          If the variable does not exist, it will be created.<br/><br/>
          
          <b>CreateGUID("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This example will update the DCO variable called "MyVariable" on the current DCO node 
          with a new GUID value. If the variable does not exist, it will be created.<br/><br/>

          <b>CreateGUID("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          rrSet("This is the GUID:+@X.MyVariable", "@X.myVariable")<br/>
          This example will update the DCO variable called "MyVariable" on the current DCO node
          with a new GUID value. The following rrSet then makes an additional update to the variable to pre-append the string
          "This is the GUID:" to the generated GUID.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True,</b> if successful, otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters and the 
        RuleRunnerLogic action library top level help for the complete smart parameter reference.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>
    

    <method name="DeleteAllCharactersAlphabetic" qi="Deletes alphabetic characters from a field or variable.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to be updated.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Deletes all alphabetic characters from the current Field object's recognized text value or a DCO variable.
        Other characters such as numbers, punctuation and symbols will remain.
        If the target is a field's recognized text, the confidence of the
        remaining characters will not be changed by this operation.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DeleteAllCharactersAlphabetic("@F")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field level object, the recognized text value
          "JAN2003" becomes "2003".<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteAllCharactersAlphabetic("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This action will delete all alphabetic characters from the variable on the current 
          DCO object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        DeleteAllCharactersNumeric, DeleteSelectedCharacters, DeleteAllCharactersMiscellaneous, DeleteAllCharactersPunctuation, DeleteAllCharactersMatchingExpression<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="DeleteAllCharactersMatchingExpression" qi="Deletes all characters in a field or variable that match a regular expression.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="RegularExpression" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to be updated.<br/>
        <b>RegularExpression:</b> A regular expression that equates to a matching string pattern.  Any characters
          that match the expression will be deleted from the text. Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Removes all characters with the target field text or target DCO variable that 
        match the provided expression.  Other characters will remain.
        If the target is a field's recognized text, the confidence of the
        remaining characters will not be changed by this operation.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DeleteAllCharactersMatchingExpression("@F", "@STRING(\p{N}+)")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field level object, it will remove
          all numeric characters from the text. Because the action supports smart
          parameters and because the input expression has a + symbol, which is
          a special smart parameter symbol, the input string needs to be specified
          using a smart parameter to prevent interpretation of the +. One possible
          way to do this is by using the @STRING() as shown.<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteAllCharactersMatchingExpression("@P\MyField", "@STRING(\p{N}+)")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a page object, it will update the text value for the child field "MyField"
          by removing all numeric characters.<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteAllCharactersMatchingExpression("@X.MyVariable", "STRING(\p{N}+)")</b><br/>
          This action will delete all numeric characters from the variable "MyVariable" on the current
          DCO object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        DeleteAllCharactersAlphabetic, DeleteSelectedCharacters, DeleteAllCharactersMiscellaneous, DeleteAllCharactersPunctuation<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>
    
    <method name="DeleteAllCharactersMiscellaneous" qi="Removes a range of ASCII values from a field or variable.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to be updated.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Removes all characters with ASCII values 123 through 191, math symbols, currency signs, dingbats, box-drawing characters, etc.
        from the current Field object's recognized value or from a DCO variable.  Other characters will remain.
        If the target is a field's recognized text, the confidence of the
        remaining characters will not be changed by this operation.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DeleteAllMiscellaneousCharacters("@F")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field level object, the recognized text value
          "100¢ = $1" becomes "100  1".<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteAllMiscellaneousCharacters("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This action will delete all miscellaneous characters from the variable "MyVariable" on the current
          DCO object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        DeleteAllCharactersAlphabetic, DeleteSelectedCharacters, DeleteAllCharactersNumeric, DeleteAllCharactersPunctuation, DeleteAllCharactersMatchingExpression<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="DeleteAllCharactersNumeric" qi="Removes all numeric characters from a field.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to be updated.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Removes all Numeric characters from 
        from the current Field object's recognized value or from a DCO variable.  Other characters will remain.
        If the target is a field's recognized text, the confidence of the
        remaining characters will not be changed by this operation.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DeleteAllCharactersNumeric("@F")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field level object, the recognized text value
          "JAN2003" becomes "JAN".<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteAllCharactersNumeric("@P\MyField")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a page object, it will update the text value for the child field "MyField"
          by removing all numeric characters.<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteAllCharactersNumeric("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This action will delete all numeric characters from the variable "MyVariable" on the current
          DCO object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        DeleteAllCharactersAlphabetic, DeleteSelectedCharacters, DeleteAllCharactersMiscellaneous, DeleteAllCharactersPunctuation, DeleteAllCharactersMatchingExpression<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="DeleteAllCharactersPunctuation" qi="Removes punctuation characters from a field or variable.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to be updated.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Removes all characters with ASCII values 33-47,58-64,91-96, and 123-191 from the
        current Field object's recognized value or from a DCO variable.  Other characters will remain.
        If the target is a field's recognized text, the confidence of the
        remaining characters will not be changed by this operation.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DeleteAllPunctuationCharacters("@F")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field level object, the recognized text value
          "!JAN." becomes "JAN".<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteAllPunctuationCharacters("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This action will delete all punctuation characters from the variable "MyVariable" on the current
          DCO object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        DeleteAllCharactersAlphabetic, DeleteSelectedCharacters, DeleteAllCharactersMiscellaneous, DeleteAllCharactersNumeric, DeleteAllCharactersMatchingExpression<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="DeleteAllCharactersSelected" qi="Deletes specific characters from a field text value.">
      <p name="CharactersToDelete" type="string" />
      <p name="StartIndex" type="string" />
      <p name="Count" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>CharactersToDelete</b> The character or string of characters to be deleted.<br/>
        <b>StartIndex</b> A number indicating the starting index within the string to start deletion.  If this parameter
        is blank or is not a number, the starting index value will default to the first position in the string.<br/>
        <b>Count</b> The number of times the character or character string is to be deleted from the
        value. The default is "1" and  "*" deletes all instances.<br/>
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Deletes specific characters from the current Field object's recognized value.
        Other characters will remain.
        The confidence of the remaining characters will not be changed by this operation.<br/><br/>

        If a single character is provided as the pattern, then every occurrence of that character will
        be deleted up to the amount specified by the count.  If multiple characters are provided,
        then that pattern must match exactly for the pattern to be deleted.<br/><br/>
        
        This action works on a field text value. To perform this operation on a DCO variable,
        a temporary field could be used by calling rrSet to populate a field with the desired text,
        run the action, then use rrSet to copy the data back to where it is needed.<br/><br/>
        
        As with any action that supports smart parameters, if the characters to delete include
        smart parameter symbols, they must be specified as a smart parameter so they are properly processed.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DeleteAllCharactersSelected("-", "","*")</b><br/>
          223-56-7669 becomes 223567669<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteAllCharactersSelected("-","5,"*")</b><br/>
          223-86-7669 becomes 223-867669<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteAllCharactersSelected(",","","2")</b><br/>
          "Hello, Roger, Tom, and Jan" becomes "Hello Roger Tom, and Jan"<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteSelectedChars("@STRING(@)","","*")</b><br/>
          "Hello, @Roger, @Tom, and @Jan" becomes "Hello, Roger, Tom, and Jan"<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteSelectedChars("@STRING(@)+Tom,","","*")</b><br/>
          "Hello, @Roger, @Tom, and @Jan" becomes "Hello, @Roger, , and @Jan"<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        DeleteAllCharactersAlphabetic, DeleteSelectedCharacters, DeleteAllCharactersMiscellaneous, DeleteAllCharactersPunctuation, DeleteAllCharactersMatchingExpression<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="DeleteAllCharactersSystem" qi="Removes control characters from a field or variable.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to be updated.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        The action removes all invisible control characters and unused code points from the 
        current Field object's recognized value or from a DCO variable.  Other characters will remain.
        If the target is a field's recognized text, the confidence of the
        remaining characters will not be changed by this operation.<br/><br/>                
        
        <e>
          <b>DeleteAllSystemCharacters("@F")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field level object, the recognized text value
          containing will be filtered to remove non-printable characters.<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteAllSystemCharacters("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This action will delete all system characters from the variable "MyVariable" on the current
          DCO object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>True.</ret>
      <see>
        DeleteAllCharactersAlphabetic, DeleteSelectedCharacters, DeleteAllCharactersMiscellaneous, DeleteAllCharactersPunctuation, DeleteAllCharactersMatchingExpression<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="DeleteLowConfidenceSpaces" qi="Removes spaces from a text field value based on their confidence.">                  
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>        
        Deletes all low confidence spaces from the current field's recognized text value. By default, any spaces with a confidence of less than 9
        is removed from the fields text value.  The default value can be changed by configuring the Setup DCO variable "ReqConf".
        This variable must be configured in the setup DCO of the object, not the runtime DCO.
        <e>
          <b>DeleteLowConfidenceSpaces()</b>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
         Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for general information regarding this action library.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="FormatNumberToLocale" qi="Guesses at the current numeric format to change a numeric value to match the current locale formatting.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        This action evaluates the current field value for known number patterns and if a known pattern is detected,
        updates the decimal and digit separators characters (if present) to match that of the current locale,
        for use when processing fields with number types formatted incorrectly for the current locale. i.e. USA vs European.<br/><br/>

        Known patterns are industry standard digits with or without 3 digit group separators, and 1-2 digits following a decimal separator.<br/><br/>

        Distinct Analyzed groups:<br/><br/>

        1) Numerals with decimal point<br/>
        Group Separators using comma, apostrophe or space characters.<br/>
        Decimal Separator using decimal character.<br/><br/>

        2) Numerals with decimal comma<br/>
        Group Separator using decimal, apostrophe or space characters.<br/>
        Decimal Separator using comma character.<br/><br/>

        3) Numerals with Arabic/Persian characters<br/>
        Group Separator using Arabic/Persian character.<br/>
        Decimal Separator using Arabic/Persian character.<br/><br/>

        Note: Numerals with lakhs layout and decimal mark (pattern of n,nn,nn,nnn.dd) are not supported.<br/><br/>
        
        The target numeric format is determined by the default number format for the locale in affect for the current DCO node.
        For example, the default United States format uses a period as a decimal separator. 
        The standard default numeric format for the current locale is always used as the target numeric format.
        A Datacap application can operate in
        a single local or multiple locales, depending upon the setup and rules of an application.
        See the section "Locale Sensitive Operations" in the top level help of this action library.<br/><br/>

        <e>
          a) Given a US number 1,234.56 to be formatted to Germany.<br/>
          <b>FormatNumberToLocale()</b><br/>
          New format will be 1.234,56<br/><br/>

          b) Given a US number 1234.5 to be formatted to Germany.<br/>
          <b>FormatNumberToLocale()</b><br/>
          New format will be 1234,5<br/><br/>

          c) Given a US number 1,234.567 to be formatted to Germany.<br/>
          <b>FormatNumberToLocale()</b><br/>
          Format will remain 1,234.567 since 3 digits following the decimal are not supported.<br/><br/>

          d) Given a US number 12345678.90 to be formatted to Germany.<br/>
          <b>FormatNumberToLocale()</b><br/>
          New format will be 12345678.90<br/><br/>

          e) Given a German number 1.234.567,89 to be formatted to US.<br/>
          <b>FormatNumberToLocale()</b><br/>
          New format will be 1,234,567.89<br/><br/>

          f) Given a German number 0,5 to be formatted to US.<br/>
          <b>FormatNumberToLocale()</b><br/>
          New format will be 0.5<br/><br/>

        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if no errors are encountered. Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the library top level help topic "Locale Sensitive Operations" for information regarding the use of different locales in an application.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="GetSubstringOccurrenceCount" qi="Counts the number of times a character, or substring, occurs within the text.">
	  <p name="TextToSearch" type="string" />
      <p name="Substring" type="string" />
      <p name="StorageVariable" type="string" />
	  <p name="CaseSensitive" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>TextToSearch:</b> The string to search for the character or string of characters.<br/><br/>
		  
		<b>Substring:</b> The character, or character string, to search for in the TextToSearch.<br/><br/>
		  
        <b>StorageVariable:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to the target DCO object or variable that will be used
		  to store the occurrence count.<br/><br/>		  
		  
		<b>CaseSensitive:</b> False, search is case insensitive. True, search is case sensitive. Defaults to True, if the value is blank or invalid.<br/><br/>
		  
		  Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
	  </ap>
      <h>		  
        The action counts the number of times the Substring is found within the TextToSearch.
		The Substring can be a single character or a string of characters.
		Typically, the TextToSearch will be a smart parameter that references a variable or field.<br/><br/>
		  
        The Substring count is stored in the specified StorageVariable location. Subsequent actions can access the variable
        and use it as required.<br/><br/>

		The StorageVariable smart parameter can reference a variable or the text value of an existing field.
		StorageVariable specifies a DCO variable that does not exist, it will be created.  If the target
		specified by StorageVariable already contains a value when this action is called, it will be overwritten by the new value.<br/><br/>
        <e>
			<b>GetSubstringOccurrenceCount("This is the test string", "t", "@X.Count", "")</b><br/>
			The example will count the number of lower case "t" characters in the string and store the value in
			a variable called "Count" on the current object.<br/><br/>

			<b>GetSubstringOccurrenceCount("AAAaA aaAAAA", "aa", "@P\Field1", "False")</b><br/>
			This example will perform a case insensitive compare looking for the word "aa" in "AAAAa aaAAAA"
			and the count will be stored in a field attached to the current page called "Field1".
			The total count will be 5, because overlaps are not counted.  The matches are
			"AA", "Aa", "aa", "AA" and "AA".<br/><br/>

			<b>GetSubstringOccurrenceCount("@P\Field1", "Total", "@P\Field1.MyCount", "False")</b><br/>
			rrCompareNumeric("@P\Field1.MyCount",">", "10")<br/>
			Here the action will search the text value in the child field object called "Field1".
			It will perform a case insensitive count for the word "Total" in the field.  The count
			total will be stored in a variable called "MyCount" on "Field1".  Then rrCompareNumeric
			will check the value of the count.  If the occurrences are more than 10, then the action will return
			True and the next action in the function will run.  If there value is 10 or less, then
			rrCompareNumeric will return False and skip to the next function in the rule.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False,</b> if an error occurs, otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        rrCompareNumeric in the RuleRunnerLogic action library.<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters and the
        RuleRunnerLogic action library top level help for the complete smart parameter reference.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

	  <method name="GetTextLength" qi="Places the numeric length of a text string into a variable.">
		  <p name="TestString" type="string" />
		  <p name="StorageVariable" type="string" />
		  <ap>
			  <b>TestString:</b> The string to calculate the length.  Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
			  <b>StorageVariable:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to the target DCO object or variable that will be used
			  to store the length of the TestString.<br/><br/>
		  </ap>
		  <h>
			  Determines the length of a the provided string, or of the value of the DCO variable specified by a smart parameter.
			  The length value is stored in the specified StorageVariable location. Subsequent actions can access the variable
			  and use it as required.<br/><br/>
			  If the target DCO variable specified in StorageVariable does not exist, it will be created.  If the target
			  specified by StorageVariable already contains a value when this action is called, it will be overwritten by the new value.<br/><br/>
			  <e>
				  <b>GetStringLength("Hello","@X.MyLength")</b><br/>
				  The example will calculate the length of the word "Hello" and store "5" in a DCO
				  variable on the current object called "MyLength".  If this example is called on a Page DCO object,
				  then the Page object will have the variable called "MyLength".  Likewise, if this example is called
				  on a Field object, then the variable "MyLength" will be created on the Field object.<br/><br/>

				  <b>GetStringLength("@X.FirstName","@X.FirstNameLength")</b><br/>
				  It is not usually useful to hard code the test string for this action, as shown in the example "Hello" above.
				  A typical use of this action will be to provide a "smart parameter" for the TestString parameter, allowing the input
				  to be a runtime variable or field.  This example assumes that the application has created a variable called "FirstName"
				  on the current DCO node prior to calling this action. The length is then stored in a variable called "FirstNameLength"
				  on the same node.<br/><br/>

				  <b>GetStringLength("@P\FirstName","@X.FirstNameLength")</b><br/>
				  This example assumes that the action is called at the page level and there is a field called "FirstName"
				  on the current DCO page. The length of the text in the field's text value is then stored in a variable called "FirstNameLength"
				  on the current page node.<br/><br/>

				  <b>GetStringLength("@P\FirstName","@P\FirstName.FirstNameLength")</b><br/>
				  This example assumes that the action is called at the page level and there is a field called "FirstName"
				  on the current DCO page. The length of the text in the field's text value is then stored in a variable called "FirstNameLength"
				  on the "FirstName" field node.<br/><br/>

				  <b>GetStringLength("@X","@X.MyLength")</b><br/>
				  rrCompareNumeric("@X.MyLength",">", "10")<br/>
				  This example assumes that the action is called on a field node and obtains the length of the text within the field and
				  stores it in a field variable called "MyLength".
				  If the length is greater than 10 characters, the next action in the current function will be called.  If this is an expected
				  condition, the following actions may do some work.  If it is an unexpected condition, the following actions may set an error.
				  If the length is less than 10, rrCompareNumeric will return false and the next function in the ruleset will run.
				  <br/><br/>
			  </e>
		  </h>
		  <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
		  <ret>
			  <b>False,</b> if an error occurs, otherwise, <b>True.</b>
		  </ret>
		  <see>
			  rrCompareNumeric in the RuleRunnerLogic action library.<br/>
			  Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters and the
			  RuleRunnerLogic action library top level help for the complete smart parameter reference.<br/>
		  </see>
	  </method>


      <method name="GetWorkstationLocale" qi="">
          <p name="StorageVariable" type="string" />
          <ap>
              <b>StorageVariable:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to the target DCO object or variable that will be used
              to store the length of the TestString.<br/><br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action determines the current locale that is in effect and writes it to a field or variable specified
              in the parameter.<br/><br/>
              
              If the locale has been set via the locale setting in the application service, or by the application
              setting the hr_locale variable, then that locale is in effect.  If the locale has not been set by either
              of these features, then the locale set in the machine's OS is in effect.<br/><br/>
              
              This action can be useful, should the application need to keep a track of the locale that is enabled.
              For example, it the locale could be obtained and then could be acted upon by the application, should
              the application need to perform different operations based on the current locale.<br/><br/>
              
              If the application has specifically set the hr_locale variable, that variable can be checked directly.
              If they hr_locale variable may not be set, then this action will check both hr_locale and the
              current machine locale and return the one that is in effect.<br/><br/>

              It is important to remember that if hr_locale is not set, actions will use the locale of the machine
              that is running the rules. For example, if the rules are being run by rulerunner, then it will use
              the locale of the machine used by rulerunner.  If a web interface is used, then it will be the configuration
              of the machine running the web service.  If DCDesktop is used, then it will be the machine running DCDesktop.
              If hr_locale is not set, then configure all of the machines to have the same base locale setting or
              rules may run with different locales, depending on the machine running the rules.<br/><br/>
              <e>
                  GetWorkstationLocale("@X.CurrentLocale");<br/>
                  rrCompareNotCase("@X.CurrentLocale", "en-US", "False")<br/>
                  SwitchMMDD("/")<br/>
                  <br/><br/>
                  
                  This example gets the current locale and stores it in a DCO variable called "CurrentLocale".
                  If the current locale is not US English, it swaps the month and day.<br/><br/>                  
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>False,</b> if an error occurs, otherwise, <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>
              Refer to the top level help for more information about Locale Sensitive Operations.<br/>
          </see>
      </method>


      <method name="InsertCharacters" qi="Inserts one or more characters into a field text value at a specific position.">
      <p name="InsertString" type="string" />
      <p name="StartIndex" type="string" />
      <p name="Count" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>InsertString:</b> The character or character string to be inserted; defaults to a space.<br/><br/>
        <b>StartIndex:</b> A number indicating the target position within the captured value;
        defaults to the end of the value.<br/><br/>
        <b>Count:</b> The number of insertions.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Inserts a character or string of characters into the field's recognized value. The string can be
        inserted one or more times.  The confidence of the existing characters will not be changed.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>InsertCharacters("=$","1","1")</b><br/>
          345.67 becomes  =$345.67<br/><br/>

          <b>InsertCharacters("=$","1","2")</b><br/>
          345.67 becomes  =$=$345.67<br/><br/>

          <b>InsertCharacters("@STRING(+$),1,1")</b><br/>
          Because smart parameters are supported, if the characters to insert
          contain a smart parameter symbol, such as '+' or '@' and it should not be interpreted as a smart parameter,
          then it needs to be specified with a smart parameter to prevent interpretation as shown.<br/>
          345.67 becomes  +$345.67<br/><br/>

          rrSet("A","@X.MyVariable")<br/>
          <b>InsertCharacters("@X.MyVariable","3","4")</b><br/>
          The value of "MyVariable" is inserted 4 times at position 3.<br/>
          abcde becomes abAAAAcde<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
    </method>

    <method name="InsertDecimalPoint" qi="Inserts the current locale's decimal separator at a specified position in a field text value.">
      <p name="Position" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Position:</b> A number indicating the character position at which to place the decimal with respect
        to the end of the string. Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Places a decimal character in the captured value, at the character position specified as a parameter.
        The parameter indicates the position of the decimal point, moving from right to left.<br/><br/>
        
        The decimal point inserted is the character designated for the current locale.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>InsertDecimalPoint("2")</b><br/>
          324556 becomes 3245.56<br/><br/>

          <b>InsertDecimalPoint("2")</b><br/>
          355 becomes 3.55<br/><br/>

          rrSet("de-DE", "@X.hr_locale")
          <b>InsertDecimalPoint("2")</b><br/>
          This action first explicitly configures the locale for the current DCO node for Germany, then InsertDecimalPoint
          inserts the correct decimal separator based on the locale, which is a comma for this locale.
          355 becomes 3,55<br/><br/>          
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level action help topic for more information regarding the use of locale in this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

      <method name="IsDocumentUsingCommaDecimalSeparators" qi="Returns true if the current document has numbers using commas as decimal separators.">      
          <ap>
              None.<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              Determines if the numeric fields in the document mostly use a comma as a decimal separator or a period as a decimal separator.
              It is required that FormatNumberToLocale has been previously called on all of the numeric fields.
              <br/>
              <e>
                  <b>IsDocumentUsingCommaDecimalSeparators()</b><br/>
                  rrSet("en-GB", "@F.hr_locale")<br/>
                  IsFieldDate()<br/><br/>
                  
                  In this example, because most numbers are using a comma as decimal separator, it assumes that the
                  date format should match the UK format.<br/><br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Any level.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              Returns <b>True</b> if most numerics use a comma as a decimal separator, otherwise <b>False</b> is returned.
          </ret>
      </method>  
      
    <method name="IsFieldCurrency" qi="Returns true if the current field's text value is a valid currency for the current locale.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Determines if the Field's captured value meets the current locale's currency format,
        including the number of decimal places, decimal and digit separator characters and
        any present currency indicators.  The locale formats are the standard currency symbol,
        digit grouping symbol and decimal symbols as defined in windows for a specific locale.<br/><br/>

        A currency symbol is not required for the validation to pass.  If a currency symbol is present,
        then the currency symbol provided must be correct for the current locale or the
        validation will fail.  Similarly, digit
        grouping symbols are not required, but if they are present, they must also be the correct symbol
        for the current locale.<br/><br/>

        If the currency has a decimal value, then the decimal symbol must be the correct symbol.  For example,
        if validating U.S. currency, and the currency has a decimal value such as 123.56, then the decimal
        separator must be the correct separator for the country.  Some countries do not allow decimal
        values in their currency, and if a decimal value is present, it will fail validation.<br/><br/>

        If the field appears to have a valid currency but it is not passing the validation, confirm
        that the locale used by the field is correct for the currency type. For example, if
        the field has a Japanese currency, then the locale must be set to Japan.  This can either be
        in effect for the whole application, such as the machine's locale, or it can be in effect
        based on the setting of the hr_locale DCO variable.<br/><br/>

        When validating Japanese currency, in addition to using the defined locale's currency
        format, it is also allowed to specify negative values using the △ symbol instead of a
        minus symbol.  Additionally, in place of a Yen symbol ¥ at the start of a currency,
        the word 円 is allowed at the end of the currency value.<br/><br/>

        If the field matches the currency format, the action returns true.  If the requirements are not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action.    Typically this would be
        called on a field object which needs to pass this validation test.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          Example 1:<br/>
          <b>IsFieldCurrency()</b><br/><br/>

          $1,200 returns <b>False</b><br/>
          $1,200.00 returns <b>True</b><br/><br/><br/>


          Example 2:<br/>
          rrSet("ja-JP","@X.hr_locale")<br/>
          <b>IsFieldCurrency()</b><br/><br/>

          This example first sets the locale for the field to Japan prior to
          validating the currency.  If hr_locale was already set at a higher DCO node level,
          such as page, document or batch, then it is not necessary to set it again
          at the field level.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the current locale's format criteria are met. Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level action help topic for more information regarding the use of locale in this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsFieldDate" qi="Determines if the current field's text value matches the current locale's default date format.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Checks that the value of the field has an acceptable Date format. Uses the current locale setting to determine
        the valid date pattern.<br/><br/>

        This action will accept any valid date from January 1st year 1 through December 31st 9999.<br/><br/>

        If the date requirements are met, the action returns true.  If the date requirements are not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsFieldDate()</b><br/><br/>

          In locale en-US (United States):<br/>

          April 6, 1944   returns <b>True</b><br/>
          04/06/44   returns <b>True</b><br/>
          30.6.44   returns <b>False</b><br/>
          Feb 31,2003   returns <b>False</b><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the field value matches the date format for the locale. Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level action help topic for more information regarding the use of locale in this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsFieldDateEqualOrAfter" qi="Returns true if the current field is equal to or after the specified date.">
      <p name="Date" type="string" />
      <ap>
        The parameter is the date that will be compared to the date of the current field.
        The source date can be a hard coded date or it can be obtained from another field value
        or a DCO variable referenced with a smart parameter.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Checks that the Date value in the current DCO field recognized text value
        is greater than or equal to the Date value in the
        DCO field or value specified as the parameter.<br/><br/>
        
        The two dates must both conform to the date format for the current locale.
        See the top level help in this action library for more information about the locale.<br/><br/>

        If the date requirements are met, the action returns true.  If the date requirements are not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsFieldDateEqualOrAfter("2/14/1964")</b><br/>
          This action compares the date in the current field's text value with the hard coded date "2/14/1964".
          If the date in the field is the same or after 2/14/1964, the action will return true.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateEqualOrAfter("@DATE")</b><br/>
          This example compares the current field's value with the current date.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateEqualOrAfter("@P\MyCompareField")</b><br/>
          Assuming the parent page object contains a sibling field called "MyCompareField", 
          this example compares the current field's value with the text value in the field
          called "MyCompareField".<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateEqualOrAfter("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This example compares the current field's value with the value of the DCO variable
          "MyVariable" in the current field object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the date condition is not met, if the action is not applied at the Field level, or either field
        does not contain a valid date. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsFieldDateEqualOrBefore, IsFieldDateWithinRange<br/>
        Refer to the top level action help topic for more information regarding the use of locale in this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsFieldDateEqualOrBefore" qi="Returns true if the current field is equal to or before the specified date.">
      <p name="Date" type="string" />
      <ap>
        The parameter is the date that will be compared to the date of the current field.
        The source date can be a hard coded date or it can be obtained from another field value
        or a DCO variable referenced with a smart parameter.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Checks that the Date value in the current DCO field recognized text value
        is less than or equal to the Date value in the
        DCO field or value specified as the parameter.<br/><br/>

        The two dates must both conform to the date format for the current locale.<br/><br/>

        If the date requirements are met, the action returns true.  If the date requirements are not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsFieldDateEqualOrBefore("2/14/1964")</b><br/>
          This action compares the date in the current field's text value with the hard coded date "2/14/1964".
          If the date in the field is the same or before 2/14/1964, the action will return true.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateEqualOrAfter("@DATE")</b><br/>
          This example compares the current field's value with the current date.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateEqualOrAfter("@P\MyCompareField")</b><br/>
          Assuming the parent page object contains a sibling field called "MyCompareField",
          this example compares the current field's value with the text value in the field
          called "MyCompareField".<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateEqualOrAfter("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This example compares the current field's value with the value of the DCO variable
          "MyVariable" in the current field object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the date condition is not met, if the action is not applied at the Field level, or either field
        does not contain a valid date. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsFieldDateEqualOrAfter, IsFieldDateWithinRange<br/>
        Refer to the top level action help topic for more information regarding the use of locale in this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsFieldDateWithinRange" qi="Returns true if the current field text value is between the specified dates.">
      <p name="StartDate" type="string" />
      <p name="EndDate" type="string" />
      <ap>        
        <b>StartDate: </b> The current field must be a validate that is the same or after this date.<br/>
        <b>EndDate:</b> The current field must be a validate that is the same or before this date.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>        
        Checks that the value assigned to the Text property of the current field is within the range specified by the parameters.<br/><br/>
        
        All of the provided dates must be in the correct format, for the current locale.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsFieldDateWithinRange("1/1/2020", "1/31/2020")</b><br/>
          This example tests that the current field's text value is between the 1/1/2020 and 1/31/2020.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateWithinRange("February 9, 1964", "January 30, 1969")</b><br/>
          This example tests that the current field's text value is between the 2/9/1964 and 1/30/1969.<br/><br/>
          
          <b>IsFieldDateWithinRange("9/12/1966", "@DATE")</b><br/>
          This example uses the smart parameter @DATE to set the end date to the current date.  If the current
          field's text value is between 9/12/1966 and the current date, then the action will return true.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateWithinRange("@P\MyField1", "@P\MyField2")</b><br/>
          This example assumes that the current field has two sibling fields on the same page
          called "MyField1" and "MyField2".  If the current field value is greater than or equal to the
          date value in MyField1 and less than or equal to the field value in MyField2, the action will return true.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateWithinRange("@X.MyVariable1", "@P\MyField1")</b><br/>
          This example assumes that the current field has a sibling field on the same page
          called "MyField1".  If the current field value is greater than or equal to the
          date value in DCO variable "MyVariable" on the current field and less than or equal to the 
          field value in MyField1, the action will return true.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field Level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the current field text is not a valid date;
        if either parameters are not validate dates; or the Text property's Date value is not within the range
        specified by the parameters.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsFieldDateEqualOrAfter, IsFieldDateEqualOrBefore<br/>
        Refer to the top level action help topic for more information regarding the use of locale in this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsFieldDateWithinXDays" qi="Returns true if the current field text value is near the specified date within the day count limit.">
      <p name="Days" type="string" />
      <ap>
        A number that specifies how many days make up the review period from the current date.<br/>
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Checks that current Field object's text value is a date that is within the number of days 
        of the current date as indicated 
        by the input parameter.  The field's text value must be a date that matches the date
        format for the current locale.<br/><br/>

        If the date requirements are met, the action returns true.  If the date requirements are not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>          
          <b>IsFieldDateWithinXDays("30")</b><br/>
          This sequence checks that a value is a date within 30 days of today's date.
          The field date can be up to 30 days earlier or 30 days later.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateWithinXDays("@P.Days")</b><br/>
          This example obtains the number of days from a DCO variable called "Days" on the
          parent page object and then compares it to the date in the current field's text
          value to confirm that it is with the number of days of today's date.  If the variable
          "Days" contained the value "45", then the action will return true if the field's value
          is a date that is within 45 days of today's date.<br/><br/>

          rrSet("@P\MyField.MyVariable", "@X")<br/> 
          <b>IsFieldDateWithinXDays("30")</b><br/>
          To use this action on a variable instead of a field, rrSet can be used to first
          populate a field with a date from a DCO variable and then the action can be run
          on the field with the date to test.  This example shows that the field
          is filled with the value of the variable "MyVariable" from the sibling
          field on the same page called "MyField". The current field text is
          populated with the value of the variable then IsFieldDateWithinXDays
          runs to perform the test.  This approach can be used with virtually
          any similar action that only operates on field text values.
          These actions must be run on a rule attached to a DCO field object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b>if the field's value is not a valid date or if the date is
        out side of the number of days in the parameter; otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsFieldDate, IsFieldDateEqualOrAfter, IsFieldDateEqualOrBefore<br/>
        Refer to the top level action help topic for more information regarding the use of locale in this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsFieldDateWithReformat" qi="Returns true if the field is a valid date that matches the current locale date format and reformats it to a new date pattern.">
      <p name="NewDateFormat" type="string" />
      <ap>
        The new date format that will be applied to the date in the current field.<br/><br/>

        mm/dd/yyyy<br/>
        mm/dd/yy<br/>
        dd/mm/yy<br/>
        mm.dd.yy, etc.<br/><br/>

        Defaults to system short date format if no format or single '*' is used.<br/>
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Reformats the date in the current field to the month, day and year format specified by the
        parameter.  The parameter can use the values "d", "m" and "y" to indicate the respective
        portions of the date and "." or "/" can be used to separate the values.
        Dates are reformatted into a short date format only.<br/><br/>

        The field's source date must be in a format that is correct for the current locale.  Once the operation
        is complete, the date may no longer conform to the format specified for the current locale.<br/><br/>

        d or m evaluates to the number without a leading zero, such as 1 to 31.<br/>
        dd or mm evaluates to the number with a leading zero, such as 01 to 31.<br/>
        ddd or mmm evaluates to the abbreviated name, such as Mon or Dec.<br/>
        dddd or mmmm evaluates to the full name such as Monday or December.<br/>        
        yy evaluates to a two digit year with a leading zero.<br/>        
        yyyy evaluates to a four digit year.<br/><br/>

        If the length requirements are met, the action returns true.  If the length requirements are not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsFieldDateWithReformat("*")</b><br/>          
          June 3, 2002 becomes 06/03/2002<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateWithReformat("yyyy/mm/dd")</b><br/>
          May 26, 1967 becomes 1967/05/26<br/><br/>

          <b>IsFieldDateWithReformat("yyyy/mm/dd")</b><br/>
          06/25/67 becomes 1967/05/26<br/><br/>
          
          <b>IsFieldDateWithReformat("mm.dd.yy")</b><br/>
          June 3, 2002 becomes 06.03.02
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the parameter is invalid, or the current field value is not a valid date given the specified format.  Otherwise <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsFieldPercentAlphabetic" qi="Returns true if a field's text value has the specified percentage of alphabetic characters">
      <p name="Percentage" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Percentage: </b> An integer from 0 to 100 indicating the percentage of characters in the field's text value that must be
        alphabetic. The value is the minimum percentage required for the action to return true.
        The parameter must be numeric, without the percent sign. If the percentage is invalid, it will default to 100.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Determines if the characters in the current field's text value are n% alphabetic.<br/><br/>

        If the percentage requirements are met, the action returns true.  If the percentage requirements are not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsFieldPercentAlpha("50")</b>   #RPR-1421 returns <b>False</b><br/>
          <b>IsFieldPercentAlpha("30")</b>   #RPR1421 returns <b>True</b>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the parameter's minimum requirement is met. Otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsFieldPercentNumeric, IsValueExactMatch<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsFieldPercentNonNumeric" qi="Returns true if a field's text value has the specified percentage of non-numeric characters">
      <p name="Percentage" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Percentage: </b> An integer from 0 to 100 indicating the percentage of characters in the field's text value that must be
        non-numeric characters. The value is the minimum percentage required for the action to return true.
        The parameter must be numeric, without the percent sign. If the percentage is invalid, it will default to 100.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Determines if the characters in the current field's text value are n% not numeric
        characters, this includes and is not limited to valid decimal and numeric separator characters.  The
        action will compare the actual percentage and confirm that it is less than or equal to the percentage
        provided by the parameter.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          Given the current value is "1,234.56US"<br/><br/>
          (Percentage of non-numeric characters is 40%)
          <b>IsFieldPercentNonNumeric("0")</b> returns <b>True</b><br/><br/>
          <b>IsFieldPercentNonNumeric("30")</b> returns <b>True</b><br/><br/>
          <b>IsFieldPercentNonNumeric("40")</b> returns <b>True</b><br/><br/>
          <b>IsFieldPercentNonNumeric("70")</b> returns <b>False</b><br/><br/>
          <b>IsFieldPercentNonNumeric("100")</b> returns <b>False</b>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the parameter's minimum requirement is met. Otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsFieldPercentAlphalphabetic, IsValueExactMatch<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsFieldPercentNumeric" qi="Returns true if a field's text value has the specified percentage of numeric characters">
      <p name="Percentage" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Percentage: </b> An integer from 0 to 100 indicating the percentage of characters in the field's text value that must be
        numeric. The value is the minimum percentage required for the action to return true.
        The parameter must be numeric, without the percent sign. If the percentage is invalid, it will default to 100.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Determines if the characters in the current field's text value are n% numeric
        characters. Does not include, and is not limited to, valid decimal and numeric separator characters.
        <e>
          Given the current field text value is "1,234.56US"<br/><br/>
          (Percentage of numeric characters is 60%)
          <b>IsFieldPercentNumeric("0")</b> returns <b>True</b><br/><br/>
          <b>IsFieldPercentNumeric("50")</b> returns <b>True</b><br/><br/>
          <b>IsFieldPercentNumeric("60")</b> returns <b>True</b><br/><br/>
          <b>IsFieldPercentNumeric("70")</b> returns <b>False</b><br/><br/>
          <b>IsFieldPercentNumeric("100")</b> returns <b>False</b>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the parameter's minimum requirement is met. Otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsFieldPercentAlpha, IsValueExactMatch<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


      <method name="IsInCharacterSet" qi="Tests that the text only contains characters within the specified list.">
          <p name="CharacterSet" type="string" />
          <p name="TextToTest" type="string" />
          <ap>
              <b>CharacterSet:</b> A list of characters.<br/><br/>
              
              <b>TextToText</b> The text to test to confirm it only contains characters from the character set.<br/><br/>
              
              Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              Validates that text in a field or in a DCO variable contains only characters from within the provided character set.
              Characters are case sensitive.<br/><br/>
              <e>                  
                  IsInCharacterSet("@X", "0123456789.,-$")<br/><br/>
                  
                  Assuming this action is called on a field object, it will return true if the text within the field is only contains
                  characters from within the provided set. If the field contains text that is not within the provided character set,
                  then the action will return false.
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Any level.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>True</b> if the referenced text contains only characters from the listed character set or if the field is empty.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>
      

      <method name="IsLocalDecimalSeparator" qi="Tests the current decimal separator.">
          <p name="TestChar" type="string" />
          <ap>
              A single character to test as the decimal separator.  Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              Tests the input decimal separator to see if it matches the default separator of the locale
              of the current DCO object.  Be aware that the locale for the current DCO object
              can be different from the locale of other objects in the hierarchy and different from
              the locale of the machine running the rules if hr_locale has been set.<br/>
              <e>
                  <b>IsLocalDecimalSeparator(",")</b><br/>
                  IsInCharacterSet("0123456789.,-$", "@X")<br/>
                  ConvertToLocalDecimal()<br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Any level.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>True</b> if the specified character is the current decimal separator.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>

    <method name="IsOMRCheckedMaximum" qi="Returns true if the current OMR field has detected selections up to the specified maximum.">
      <p name="OMRLimit" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>OMRLimit:</b> Integer value specifying the maximum number of OMR selections that are allowed.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>        
        This action confirms that the number of OMR (Optical Mark Recognition) selections
        is less than or equal to the number passed as a parameter.  An OMR is typically a circle or box shape
        that is either filled in or not filled in.  The number of selections that are filled in cannot
        exceed the value provided as a parameter. If the number of filled in selections is less than
        or equal to the parameter, then this action will return true.  If the selected count it
        greater than the parameter, this action will return false.<br/><br/>

        This action must be set on a field that is configured as an OMR field.  An OMR field must have
        the DCO variable "RecogType" set to "4".  An OMR field must also be setup properly to be correctly
        recognized and processed as an OMR field.<br/><br/>

        The OMR field has a sub-field for each OMR selection.  When the OMR is recognized,
        the parent OMR field will have a text value of 0's and 1's, each corresponding to the sub-fields.
        A "0" indicates the OMR was not considered as filled in and a "1" considers the OMR was filled in.
        This action will check the text value of the OMR field to determine if the number of 1's meets
        the parameter's requirements.<br/><br/>

        If the maximum requirement is exceeded, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsOMRCheckedMaximum("1")</b><br/>
          This action confirms that only 1 of the available OMR selections has been filled in.  One
          example would be a selection of the type of credit card, only one credit card should be
          selected.  If multiple credit cards are selected, then this can be used to flag the OMR field as an
          error so a verify operator can review the selection.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsOMRCheckedMaximum("1")</b><br/>
          IsOMRCheckedMinimum("1")<br/>
          This set of actions combines two requirements to ensure that 1 and only 1 OMR has been selected.  If these
          both pass, then the selection count is valid.  If one of the tests fails, then it can be flagged
          for a verify operator to review the problem in a verify panel.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsOMRCheckedMaximum("@X.MaxAllowed")</b><br/>
          This example uses a smart parameter to obtain the required maximum from a DCO variable called "MaxAllowed"
          on the same DCO object that is running the action.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>An OMR Field.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the parameter you enter is not numeric, or the field is not an OMR field.<br/><br/>

        <b>True</b> if the number of OMR boxes checked is less than or equal to the parameter you entered.
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsOMRCheckedMinimum<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="IsOMRCheckedMinimum" qi="Returns true if the current OMR field has detected selections of the specified minimum value.">
      <p name="OMRLimit" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>OMRLimit:</b> Integer value specifying the minimum number of OMR selections that are allowed.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action confirms that the number of OMR (Optical Mark Recognition) selections
        is greater than or equal to the number passed as a parameter.  An OMR is typically a circle or box shape
        that is either filled in or not filled in.  The number of selections that are filled in cannot
        exceed the value provided as a parameter. If the number of filled in selections is greater than
        or equal to the parameter, then this action will return true.  If the selected count it
        less than the parameter, this action will return false.<br/><br/>

        This action must be set on a field that is configured as an OMR field.  An OMR field must have
        the DCO variable "RecogType" set to "4".  An OMR field must also be setup properly to be correctly
        recognized and processed as an OMR field.<br/><br/>

        The OMR field has a sub-field for each OMR selection.  When the OMR is recognized,
        the parent OMR field will have a text value of 0's and 1's, each corresponding to the sub-fields.
        A "0" indicates the OMR was not considered as filled in and a "1" considers the OMR was filled in.
        This action will check the text value of the OMR field to determine if the number of 1's meets
        the parameter's requirements.<br/><br/>

        If the minimum requirement is not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsOMRCheckedMinimum("1")</b><br/>
          This action confirms that only 1 of the available OMR selections has been filled in.  One
          example would a form that has multiple selections and one or more selections can be chosen, but at least
          one of them must be selected.  This action will confirm that there is at least one OMR selected
          out of the available selections.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsOMRCheckedMinimum("1")</b><br/>
          IsOMRCheckedMaximum("1")<br/>
          This set of actions combinds two requirements to ensure that 1 and only 1 OMR has been selected.  If these
          both pass, then the selection count is valid.  If one of the tests fails, then it can be flagged
          for a verify operator to review the problem in a verify panel.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsOMRCheckedMinimum("@X.MinAllowed")</b><br/>
          This example uses a smart parameter to obtain the required maximum from a DCO variable called "MinAllowed"
          on the same DCO object that is running the action.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>An OMR Field.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the parameter you enter is not numeric, or the field is not an OMR field.<br/><br/>

        <b>True</b> if the number of OMR boxes checked is less than or equal to the parameter you entered.
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsOMRCheckedMaximum<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    
    <method name="IsRegularExpressionPatternInValue" qi="Returns true if a field or variable has a pattern that matches the regular expression.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="RegularExpression" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field, DCO variable or string to validate.<br/><br/>
        
        <b>RegularExpression:</b> The expression can include any Regular Expression characters.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action validates the value Target parameter to see if it meets the requirements
        of the provided regular expression.<br/><br/>
        
        If you are using an input boundary, ^, it must be followed by a space and then the remainder
        of the search string.<br/><br/>

        If the regular expression is matched, the action returns true.  If the regular expression is not matched, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action, so it is typically useful to call this action on the DCO object
        that is being checked so the message is associated with the same object.  Typically this would be
        called on a field object which needs to pass this validation test.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsRegularExpressionPatternInValue("@F", "[\^\b\s\n\r]*Inv[oO0][iItl1]ce[\b\s]*")</b><br/>                    
          Assuming this action is attached to a field object, 
          this example searches for the word “Invoice” in the text value of the current field.
          To allow for recognition errors, the search allows for common recognition
          substitutions in “o” and “i” by matching “Invoice”, “InvOice”, “Inv0ice”,
          “Inv01ce”, “Involce”, etc.  The search will also ignore any text placed before or after the word.<br/><br/>          

          <b>IsRegularExpressionPatternInValue("@F", "@STRING([\^\b\s\n\r]*Inv[oO0][iItl1]ce[\b\s]*)")</b><br/>
          Because the action supports smart parameters, if the regular expression contains
          smart parameter identifiers like @ or +, then those parameters will need to be passed in
          a way to prevent interpretation as smart parameter identifiers.
          Typically the easiest way to achieve this is by encapsulating the parameter
          with the smart parameter @STRING() or @CHR().<br/><br/>

          <b>IsRegularExpressionPatternInValue("@F", "@X.MyExpression")</b><br/>
          This example is similar to the previous examples but obtains the regular expression
          pattern from a DCO variable called "MyExpression" on the current object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All, but generally at the Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the pattern is found within the field.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

      <method name="IsUSZipCode" qi="Checks to see if the postal code follows a US format.">
          <p name="PostalCode" type="string" qi="Smart parameters are supported."/>
          <ap>
              The zip code to test for a US zip code pattern.
          </ap>
          <h>
              Checks to see if the postal code follows a US format.  If the format of the ZIP
              fits a US format, then it assumes the document is of US origin.<br/><br/>

              Allowed formats:<br/>
              nnnnn<br/>
              nnnnnnnnn<br/>
              nnnnn-nnnn<br/><br/>
              
              <e>
                  <b>IsUSZipCode(@P\Remittance_Zip)</b><br/>
                  rrSet("en-US", "@F.hr_locale")<br/>
                  IsFieldDate()<br/>
                  IsFieldDateWithReformat("yyyy/mm/dd")<br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Any level.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              Returns <b>True</b> if the test for a US ZIP code format passes, otherwise <b>False.</b><br/>
          </ret>
      </method>
        
      


    <method name="IsValuePercentFuzzy" qi="Returns true if the current field text value matches a pattern using common substitution characters.">
      <p name="Source" type="string" />
      <p name="CompareTo" type="string" />
      <p name="PerformFuzzyCompare" type="string" />
      <p name="MinimumPercentage" type="string" />
      <p name="MatchByWord" type="string" />      
      <ap>
        <b>Source:</b> The source field, DCO variable or string that will be tested.  This is the field with a value to be compared
        to see if it meets the required minimum percentage.<br/><br/>

        <b>CompareTo:</b> The source field, DCO variable or string that will be used to compare against the source value.<br/><br/>

        <b>PerformFuzzyCompare</b> A boolean flag determines if the comparison to the source string will be a
        "fuzzy" comparison.  A "fuzzy" comparison will compare text using common substitution characters
        that can occur from recognition mistakes.  The value "True" will enable fuzzy comparison.<br/><br/>

        <b>MinimumPercentage</b> Numeric value of the percentage of required precision when matching
        by word. Numbers less than
        "100" permit the comparison to pass by requiring a smaller percentage of words to match.  This value
        must be an integer between 0 and 100.  If the value is invalid, it will default to 100.
        When MatchByWord is false, then the entire target string is considered a single word
        and must match the compare string 100%, but still respecting the fuzzy compare setting.<br/><br/>

        <b>MatchByWord</b> A boolean value of True or False which indicates if the comparison should be
        a word-by-word comparison vs. a character comparison. True will enable a word by word
        comparison, False compares a character comparison.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Compares an entire line of text or each word within the line, to a provided pattern.  The pattern can be matched
        "fuzzy" meaning that the patter can match if specific characters are interchanged with commonly substituted
        recognition characters.<br/><br/>

        When ByWord is false, the entire line of text from the source field text or DCO variable will be compared to the
        comparison pattern.  The entire string of characters must match the comparison string.  If PerformFuzzyCompare
        is true, then the pattern does not need to match exactly if common substitution characters are within the source string.
        The percentage parameter is not relevant when ByWord is False.<br/><br/>

        When MatchByWord is true, then the provided comparison text will be checked against each word within the source string.
        A word is considered as a string of characters separated by a space.  Each of these words will be compared to the
        comparison parameter and must match exactly.  If the PerformFuzzyCompare parameter is True, then the comparison will
        allow commonly substituted characters to match, so the text does not need to be an exact match.  The percentage value determines
        the percentage of the words that must match the comparison string.  If the source string has 10 words and 2 of the words
        match the comparison pattern, then it is considered to be a 20% match.<br/><br/>

        When performing a "fuzzy" compare, the action will use common recognition substitutions to determine if the text matches the required
        text.  For example, when fuzzy comparisons are enabled a word like "Sam" will match "5am", or "He110" will match "Hello".
        The following are common substitutions that will match when fuzzy comparisons are enabled: B8, Z2, S5, o0O and ilt1I.
        Enabling the fuzzy comparison will allow these commonly substituted characters to match the provided pattern and still
        be evaluated as an exact match.<br/><br/>

        If the comparison requirements are met, the action returns true.  If the comparison requirements are not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsValuePercentFuzzy("@F", "Hello", "True", "100", "False")</b><br/>
          Assuming this is attached to a DCO field, it will compare the text value
          of the field with the pattern "Hello".  Fuzzy matching is enabled. The following field
          values will have these return values:<br/>
          "Hello" = True<br/>
          "He11o" = True<br/>
          "He110" = True<br/>
          "Hel1O" = True<br/>
          "HELLO" = False<br/><br/>
          
          <b>IsValuePercentFuzzy("@F", "@P\OtherField", "True", "100", "False")</b><br/>
          Similar to the above example, however the comparison pattern will be obtained from the
          text value of the sibling field "OtherField".  A fuzzy comparison will be performed
          using OtherField as the pattern.<br/><br/>
          
          <b>IsValuePercentFuzzy("@F", "@DATE(DD\MM\YYYY)", "True", "100", "False")</b><br/>
          This example will compare the current field to the current date and use fuzzy matching.
          Assuming the current date is 01/01/2025, the following field values will have these return values:<br/>
          "0l/01/20Z5" = True<br/>
          "01/01/2025" = True<br/>
          "01/07/2025" = False<br/>
          "0l/0l/20ZS" = True<br/><br/>

          <b>IsValuePercentFuzzy("@F", "@P.MyVariable", "True", "25", "True")</b><br/>
          This example will compare the text value of the current field with the comparison value
          that is in the parent page object variable called "MyVariable".  Assuming the text
          value of the current field has the text "He110 Tom and Sally" and the value of "MyVariable" is
          "Hello", then the 1 of 4 words will match the pattern with the result of 25% of the
          words matching and the action will return true.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All but usually field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True,</b> if the pattern matches the source based on the matching requirements, otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    
    
    <method name="IsTextLength" qi="Returns true if the text in a field or variable satisfies a length condition.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="Length" type="string" />
      <p name="ComparisonOperator" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to validate.<br/><br/>
        
        <b>Length:</b> A numeric value to use for the length comparison. If not provided, defaults to 1.<br/><br/>
        
        <b>ComparisonOperator:</b> The comparison operator. It must be one of the following operators: ˂, ˂=, =, ˃=, ˃<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action checks the text length to confirm if it meets a minimum or maximum length requirement.
        It will determine if the text length of the "Target", typically a field value, is less than, less than or equal to,
        equal to, greater than or equal to, or greater than the length specified in the length parameter.<br/><br/>
        
        This action is useful for validation of recognized data to confirm it meets requirements. It also
        will confirm if data entered by a verify operator has entered data that meets the length requirements.<br/><br/>        
        
        If the length requirements are met, the action returns true.  If the length requirements are not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action, so it is typically useful to call this action on the DCO object
        that is being checked so the message is associated with the same object.<br/><br/>

        The action will also store the actual length of the tested text in a DCO variable called "TextLength".
        If needed, this variable can be optionally used to obtain the length of the text that was tested and used
        in other actions. The variable is created regardless of the comparison test passing or failing.
        It is not required to use this variable, it is provided should an application need to determine
        the actual length of the text for use in a subsequent task.<br/><br/>

        Because the target specification supports smart parameters, the target can be a field's text value, a DCO variable
        or hard coded text.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsTextLength("@F", "10", "˂=")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a rule attached to a field object, it will test the field's
          text value to confirm the length of the text is less than or equal to 10 characters.  The action will
          return true if the test passes, otherwise the action will return false and control will pass to the next function
          in the ruleset. The actual length of the string will also be stored in the current DCO variable "TextLength"
          for optional use.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsTextLength("@F", "0", "=")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a rule attached to a field object, it will test the field's
          text value to confirm the field is empty. The action will return false if the field is
          not empty and set a user message.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsTextLength("@F", "0", ">")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a rule attached to a field object, it will test the field's
          text value to confirm the field is not empty. The action will return false and set
          a message if it is empty.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsTextLength("@X.MyVariable", @X.LengthToTest, "=")</b><br/>
          This example uses a smart parameter to test the text contained in the DCO variable "MyVariable"
          on the same DCO object that is attached to the ruleset.  It also uses a smart parameter
          to obtain the expected length from another variable called "LengthToTest".  If the
          text contained in MyVariable is equal to the number of characters indicated
          by the numeric value of the variable LengthToTest, then the action will return true.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level but typically at the field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the comparison meets the length requirement; Otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsValueLessOrEqual" qi="Returns true if a numeric comparison on a field or variable is less than or equal to the comparison value.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="ComparisonValue" type="string" />      
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field, DCO variable or string to validate.<br/><br/>

        <b>ComparisonValue:</b> A numeric value to use for the comparison to the value of the "Target" parameter.
        The target and the comparison value numbers must be properly formatted for the current locale.<br/><br/>        

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action performs a numeric compare of the value of the "Target" parameter to the "ComparisonValue".
        It will determine if the "Target", typically a field value, is less than, less than or equal to,
        equal to, greater than or equal to, or greater than the comparison value.<br/><br/>

        This action is very similar to IsValueLessOrGreater.  The action IsValueLessOrGreater has
        greater flexibility.<br/><br/>

        This action is useful for validation of recognized data to confirm it meets requirements. It also
        will confirm if data entered by a verify operator has entered data that meets the numeric requirements.<br/><br/>

        If the comparison requirement is met, the action returns true.  If the comparison requirement is not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action, so it is typically useful to call this action on the DCO object
        that is being checked so the message is associated with the same object.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsValueLessOrEqual("@F", "10.50")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a rule attached to a field object, it will test the field's
          text value to confirm the numeric value of the text is less than or equal to 10.5.  The action will
          return true if the test passes, otherwise the action will return false and control will pass to the next function
          in the ruleset.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level but typically at the field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the two values meet the comparison requirement; Otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsValueLessOrGreater<br/>
        rrCompareNumeric in the RuleRunnerLogic action library<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>
    
    <method name="IsValueLessOrGreater" qi="Returns true if a numeric comparison on a field or variable meets the condition for the comparison value.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="ComparisonValue" type="string" />
      <p name="ComparisonOperator" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field, DCO variable or string to validate.<br/><br/>

        <b>ComparisonValue:</b> A numeric value to use for the comparison to the value of the "Target" parameter. 
        The target and the comparison value numbers must be properly formatted for the current locale.<br/><br/>

        <b>ComparisonOperator:</b> The comparison operator. It must be one of the following operators: ˂, ˂=, =, ˃=, ˃
        The comparison is applied in this format "Target" "Comparison" "Value" such as "is Target ˂= 5".<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action performs a numeric compare of the value of the "Target" parameter to the "ComparisonValue".
        It will determine if the "Target", typically a field value, is less than, less than or equal to,
        equal to, greater than or equal to, or greater than the comparison value.<br/><br/>
        
        This action is useful for validation of recognized data to confirm it meets requirements. It also
        will confirm if data entered by a verify operator has entered data that meets the numeric requirements.<br/><br/>

        If the comparison requirement is met, the action returns true.  If the comparison requirement is not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action, so it is typically useful to call this action on the DCO object
        that is being checked so the message is associated with the same object.<br/><br/>
        
        If the goal is to perform a numeric comparison to control logic flow but the message variable
        does not need to be set if the comparison fails, then consider using the rrCompareNumeric action
        in the RuleRunnerLocic action library.
        <e>
          <b>IsValueLessOrGreater("@F", "10.50", "˂=")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a rule attached to a field object, it will test the field's
          text value to confirm the numeric value of the text is less than or equal to 10.5.  The action will
          return true if the test passes, otherwise the action will return false and control will pass to the next function
          in the ruleset.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsValueLessOrGreater("@F", "0", "=")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a rule attached to a field object, it will test the field's
          text value to confirm the numeric value is zero.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsValueLessOrGreater("@F", "0", ">")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a rule attached to a field object, it will test the field's
          text value to confirm the value is greater than zero.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsValueLessOrGreater("@X.MyVariable", @X.ExpectedValue, "=")</b><br/>
          This example uses a smart parameter to test the text contained in the DCO variable "MyVariable"
          on the same DCO object that is attached to the ruleset.  It also uses a smart parameter
          to obtain the test value from another variable called "ExpectedValue".  If the
          numeric value of text contained in ExpectedValue is equal to the numeric value 
          the variable MyVariable, then the action will return true.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level but typically at the field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the two values meet the comparison requirement; Otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        rrCompareNumeric in the RuleRunnerLogic action library<br/><br/>
        
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsValueExactMatch" qi="Returns true if the field or value matches the comparison value with our without case sensitivity.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="Value" type="string" />
      <p name="CaseSensitive" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target: </b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field, DCO variable or string to validate.<br/><br/>
        <b>Value:</b> Text that the action is looking for in the current field.<br/><br/>
        <b>CaseSensitive:</b> Determines if the comparison should be case sensitive. Use values "True" or "False".
        If the parameter is left blank, it defaults to "True".<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        The action will check the text referenced by the Target parameter to validate if the text in the
        Value parameter is identical to the Target's text.  The Value must exactly match
        the input text.  If the case sensitive parameter
        is true then the sub-string must also exactly match the case of the comparison text.<br/><br/>
        
        If the comparison requirement is met, the action returns true.  If the comparison requirement is not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action, so it is typically useful to call this action on the DCO object
        that is being checked so the message is associated with the same object.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsValueExactMatch("@F", "NEW", "")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a ruleset attached to a field object, it will compare the
          text value of the current field with the word "NEW" to confirm if the word "NEW"
          is contained in the source field.<br/>
          The following are return values based on the field's text value:<br/>
          Field contains: "NEW" = TRUE<br/>
          Field contains: "I Love NEW YORK" = FALSE<br/>
          Field contains: "Project" = FALSE<br/>
          Field contains: "NEW Project" = FALSE<br/>
          Field contains: "New Project" = FALSE<br/>
          Field contains: "Development" = FALSE<br/><br/>

          <b>IsValueExactMatch("@F", "@P.MyVariable", "False")</b><br/>
          This example will compare the text value of the current DCO field with
          the value of the variable "MyVariable" which is on the parent page
          object.  The comparison will be case insensitive.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All, but generally at the Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the object's target does not contain the parameter's value. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsFieldPercentAlpha, IsFieldPercentNumeric<br/><br/>
        
        rrCompare, rrCompareNot, rrCompareCase, rrCompareCaseLength, rrCompareNotCase and rrCompareNotCaseLength in the RuleRunnerLogic action library.<br/><br/>        
        
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters and in the top level help of the RuleRunnerLogic action library.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsValueInText" qi="Returns true if the specified text is a substring text in a field or variable">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="Value" type="string" />
      <p name="CaseSensitive" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target: </b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field, DCO variable or string to validate.<br/><br/>
        <b>Value:</b> Text that the action is looking for in the current field.<br/><br/>
        <b>CaseSensitive:</b> Determines if the comparison should be case sensitive. Use values "True" or "False".
        If the parameter is left blank, it defaults to "True".<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        The action will check the text referenced by the Target parameter to validate if the text in the
        Value parameter is a subset of text contained within the Target's text.  The Value can exactly match
        the length the input text or can be a portion of the input text.  If the case sensitive parameter
        is true then the substring must exactly match the case of the comparison text.<br/><br/>

        If the comparison requirement is met, the action returns true.  If the comparison requirement is not met, the
        action returns false and also sets a corresponding error message in the standard Message variable that
        is used by clients to display in verification panels when a rule check fails. The message is set on
        the DCO that is calling this action, so it is typically useful to call this action on the DCO object
        that is being checked so the message is associated with the same object.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsValueInText("@F", "NEW", "")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a ruleset attached to a field object, it will compare the
          text value of the current field with the word "NEW" to confirm if the word "NEW"
          is contained in the source field.<br/>
          The following are return values based on the field's text value:<br/>
          Field contains: "NEW" = TRUE<br/>
          Field contains: "I Love NEW YORK" = TRUE<br/>
          Field contains: "Project" = FALSE<br/>
          Field contains: "NEW Project" = TRUE<br/>
          Field contains: "New Project" = FALSE<br/>
          Field contains: "Development" = FALSE<br/><br/>

          <b>IsValueInText("@F", "@P.MyVariable", "False")</b><br/>
          This example will compare the text value of the current DCO field with
          the value of the variable "MyVariable" which is on the parent page
          object.  The comparison will be case insensitive.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All, but generally at the Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the object's target does not contain the parameter's value. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        IsFieldPercentAlpha, IsFieldPercentNumeric<br/><br/>

        rrContains, rrCompare, rrCompareNot, rrCompareCase, rrCompareCaseLength, rrCompareNotCase and rrCompareNotCaseLength in the RuleRunnerLogic action library.<br/><br/>

        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters and in the top level help of the RuleRunnerLogic action library.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="ReplaceBlankFieldWithCharacter" qi="If the current field is blank, the specified character will be placed into the field.">
      <p name="Replacement"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Replacement:</b> A character or string that will be placed into the field if it is blank.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        If a field is empty or only contains spaces, it is replaced with the character or string that is passed in as a parameter.
        If the field is replaced with the input parameter, the confidence of the new replacement text is set to a low confidence of 1.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>ReplaceBlankFieldWithCharacter("~")</b><br/>
          This example will replace the current field with the character "~", only if the field is empty or only
          contains spaces.<br/><br/>

          <b>ReplaceBlankFieldWithCharacter("@B.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          If the field is empty or only contains spaces, it will be replaced with the text in 
          the batch level variable "MyVariable".<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>
        Field level.
      </lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if it is called at the wrong level or if the parameter is missing, otherwise <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>
    
    <method name="ReplaceCharacters" qi="Replaces specific characters in a field to a replacement character.">
      <p name="Find" type="string" />
      <p name="Replacement" type="string" />
      <p name="Count" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Find:</b> The character or string of characters to be replaced; defaults to a space.<br/><br/>

        <b>Replacement:</b> The character(s) of the replacement String.<br/><br/>

        <b>Count:</b> The number of times replacement is to occur. The default is "1"	and  "*" replaces all instances.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action will update the text of the current field object by replacing the matched character
        or string of characters with the replacement string.  The number of
        replacement occurrences is controlled by the Count parameter.<br/><br/>
        
        The confidence values of the characters that are not replaced will not be changed, keeping
        their original confidence values.  Replacement characters will be added with the default
        passing confidence value.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>ReplaceCharacters(".","/","*")</b><br/>
          If the text value of the field has a starting value of
          "02.09.1964", it will become "02/09/1964".<br/><br/>
          
          <b>ReplaceCharacters("", "@EMPTY", "*")</b><br/>
          If the source field contains "Remove The Spaces Here", the final result will be "RemoveTheSpacesHere".<br/><br/>

          <b>ReplaceCharacters("@X.FindThis", "@X.Replacement", "1")</b><br/>
          This example will look for the text indicated by the DCO variable "FindThis" and replace
          the first occurrence with the value of the variable "Replacement" on the same DCO object.
          If "FindThis" is set to "oo", "Replacement" is set to "a" and the starting
          field value is "Moon to Moose", the final field value will be "Man to Moose".<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

      <method name="ReplaceSubstringFirst" qi="Replaces the First occurance of a substring in a text string with a new substring.">
          <p name="TextToSearch" type="string" />
          <p name="Substring" type="string" />
          <p name="Replacement" type="string" />
          <p name="CaseSensitive" type="string" />
          <ap>
              <b>TextToSearch:</b> A smart parameter syntax that references a DCO field or variable to search and update.<br/><br/>

              <b>Substring:</b> The character, or character string, to search for in the resolved value of TextToSearch.<br/><br/>

              <b>Replacement:</b> The character, or character string, to replace the Substring.<br/><br/>

              <b>CaseSensitive:</b> False, search is case insensitive. True, search is case sensitive. Defaults to True, if the value is blank or invalid.<br/><br/>

              Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action will search the text of the field or variable referenced in the TextToSearch parameter.
              The first occurrence of the specified Substring will be replaced with Replacement parameter and
              the location referenced by TextToSearch will be updated.
              <br/><br/>

              If the smart parameter specified in TextToSearch resolves to a field's "text" value, this action
              will maintain the existing confidence values of the characters that are not replaced.
              The replacement characters will be set to a high confidence value.  If TextToSearch
              resolves to a DCO variable, then the confidence value does not apply.
              <br/><br/>
              The Replacement parameter is not required to be the same length as the substring.
              <br/><br/>
              <e>
                  <b>ReplaceSubstringFirst(@F, "TOO", "Three", "True")</b><br/>
                  Because the specification is @F, this action would be run on a field to update the captured text in the field.
                  If the text value of the field has a value of "This Too is TOO Much Text Too", then the final value
                  of the field will be "This Too is Three Much Text Too", because this is a case sensitive search.
                  if the confidence of the field was "55555555555555555555555555555", after updating the text value
                  the field confidence would become "5555555555559999955555555555555".<br/><br/>

                  rrSet("This Too is TOO Much Text Too", "@X.MyVariable")<br/>
                  <b>ReplaceSubstringFirst(@X.MyVariable, "TOO", "Three", "false")</b><br/>
                  The example first sets a DCO variable on the current DCO object to the value "This Too is TOO Much Text Too".
                  This is performed as an illustration. Typically the variable would have been set earlier.
                  The action ReplaceSubstringFirst will update the variable "MyVariable" to
                  "This Three is TOO Much Text Too".<br/><br/>

                  <b>ReplaceSubstringFirst(@P\Field1, ".", ",", "False)</b><br/>
                  It is assumed that this action is running on a page object that has an existing child field called "Field1".
                  The action will update the text value of Field1.
                  If the text value of the field has a value of "1.234.56", then the final value
                  of the field will be "1,234.56".
                  if the confidence of the field was "72387279", after updating the text value
                  the field confidence would become "79387279".<br/><br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>
              Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
          </see>
      </method>

	  <method name="ReplaceSubstringLast" qi="Replaces the last occurance of a substring in a text string with a new substring.">
		  <p name="TextToSearch" type="string" />
		  <p name="Substring" type="string" />
		  <p name="Replacement" type="string" />
		  <p name="CaseSensitive" type="string" />
		  <ap>
			  <b>TextToSearch:</b> A smart parameter syntax that references a DCO field or variable to search and update.<br/><br/>
			  
			  <b>Substring:</b> The character, or character string, to search for in the resolved value of TextToSearch.<br/><br/>

			  <b>Replacement:</b> The character, or character string, to replace the Substring.<br/><br/>

			  <b>CaseSensitive:</b> False, search is case insensitive. True, search is case sensitive. Defaults to True, if the value is blank or invalid.<br/><br/>
			  
			  Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
		  </ap>
		  <h>
              This action will search the text of the field or variable referenced in the TextToSearch parameter.
              The last occurrence of the specified Substring will be replaced with Replacement parameter and
              the location referenced by TextToSearch will be updated.
              <br/><br/>

              If the smart parameter specified in TextToSearch resolves to a field's "text" value, this action
              will maintain the existing confidence values of the characters that are not replaced.
              The replacement characters will be set to a high confidence value.  If TextToSearch
              resolves to a DCO variable, then the confidence value does not apply.
              <br/><br/>
              The Replacement parameter is not required to be the same length as the substring.
              <br/><br/>
			  <e>                  
				  <b>ReplaceSubstringLast(@F, "TOO", "Three", "True")</b><br/>
                  Because the specification is @F, this action would be run on a field to update the captured text in the field.
                  If the text value of the field has a value of "This Too is TOO Much Text Too", then the final value
                  of the field will be "This Too is Three Much Text Too", because this is a case sensitive search.
                  if the confidence of the field was "55555555555555555555555555555", after updating the text value
                  the field confidence would become "5555555555559999955555555555555".<br/><br/>

                  rrSet("This Too is TOO Much Text Too", "@X.MyVariable")<br/>
                  <b>ReplaceSubstringLast(@X.MyVariable, "TOO", "Three", "false")</b><br/>
                  The example first sets a DCO variable on the current DCO object to the value "This Too is TOO Much Text Too".
                  This is performed as an illustration. Typically the variable would have been set earlier.
                  The action ReplaceSubstringLast will update the variable "MyVariable" to
                  "This Too is TOO Much Text Three".<br/><br/>           

				  <b>ReplaceSubstringLast(@P\Field1, ",", ".", "False)</b><br/>
                  It is assumed that this action is running on a page object that has an existing child field called "Field1".
                  The action will update the text value of Field1.
                  If the text value of the field has a value of "1,234,56", then the final value
                  of the field will be "1,234.56".
                  if the confidence of the field was "72387279", after updating the text value
                  the field confidence would become "72387979".<br/><br/>				  
			  </e>
		  </h>
		  <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
		  <ret>
			  Always <b>True.</b>
		  </ret>
		  <see>
			  Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
		  </see>
	  </method>

    <method name="ReplaceValueAtPosition" qi="Replaces a character in a field at a specific position with a replacement character.">
      <p name="Position" type="string" />
      <p name="Replacement" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Position:</b> An integer identifying the position within the text that contains the value to be replaced.<br/><br/>
        <b>Replacement:</b> The replacement string; if blank this parameter defaults to "" indicating a deletion.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action replaces a single character at the indicated position within the string
        with a replacement value.  The replacement value can be 0 or more characters.  If the value is empty,
        then the replacement acts as a deletion. The single character that is replaced can be replaced
        with multiple characters. Only the character at the specified index is replaced.<br/><br/>

        The confidence values of the remaining characters is not changed by this action.  The
        added characters will be given a default passing confidence.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>ReplaceValueAtPosition("3","/")</b><br/>
          The field value "10.05.1962" becomes "10/05.1962".<br/><br/>

          <b>ReplceValueAtPosition("5","Hello")</b><br/>
          The field value "1234567890" becomes "1234Hello67890".<br/><br/>

          <b>ReplceValueAtPosition("5","")</b><br/>
          The field value "1234567890" becomes "123467890".<br/><br/>

          <b>ReplceValueAtPosition("@X.Index","@P.MyText")</b><br/>
          This example will obtain the start position from the DCO variable called "Index"
          on the current DCO field object. The replacement string will be obtained
          from the variable "MyText" which would exist on the parent page object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the character replacement is successful. Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


      <method name="SetAllFieldLabels" access="public" qi="Sets the label display variables from an INI file.">
          <p name="INIFileName" type="string" qi="The full path to the INI file containing the field labels." />
          <p name="AddMissingEntries" type="string" qi="True adds the missing entries into the INI file, False will not." />
          <ap>
              Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action is an automated way of updating fields with a user friendly display label at runtime.
              To perform the update, it is required that the fields and their display labels are listed in an INI file.
              Once set, the user label is displayed on verification panels instead of the field name.
              The label is supported by Navigator, Datacap Desktop and Taskmaster Web, provided the panel in use
              supports this feature.<br/><br/>

              The action will read scan through every document, page and field within the batch to set the "label"
              variable on each field in the batch. This action might take some time in large batches.  This is not the only
              way that an application can set the label variable for fields. For environments that typically have
              large batches, it would be more efficient to set the label variable
              in the setup DCO, making it permanently set without having to use this action to read the labels over and over from a file at runtime
              for every batch.<br/><br/>

              This action is not compatible with, nor is it necessary, on applications based on Flex technology.<br/><br/>

              The settings file needs to follow the following format:<br/>
              [Verification Labels]<br/>
              FieldName1=DisplayLabel<br/>
              FieldName2=DisplayLabel<br/>
              etc.<br/><br/>
              
              If AddMissingEntries is set to True, then any fields within the batch that do not exist in the INI file list is
              added to the INI file. This may make it easier to go back and edit the INI file to configure these fields.
              When the action updates the INI file, it requires that the user running the rules has full access to update
              the specified INI file. If the user running the task is Rulerunner, then the Rulerunner user must have correct
              permissions.  If the user is running the rule through a client like Datacap Desktop, then that user must have correct
              access permissions for the INI file.<br/><br/>
              <e>
                  An example INI file configuration:<br/><br/>

                  [Verification Labels]<br/>
                  Vendor=Vendor<br/>
                  Vendor_Number=Vendor Number<br/>
                  Remittance_Zip=Postal code<br/>
                  Invoice_Number=Invoice Number<br/>
                  Invoice_Date=Invoice Date<br/>
                  PO_Number=PO Number<br/>
                  Shipping=Shipping<br/><br/>
              </e>
              <br/><br/>
              <e>
                  SetLabels("@APPPATH(settingsfile)")<br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Any level, but will only process fields that are descendants of the object upon which you place the action, and the object itself  (if it is a field object).
              Typically, this action would be called at the batch level to ensure that all fields are set within the batch.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>
      

      <method name="SetFieldStatusMessage" qi="A message that appears in verify clients">
          <p name="Message" type="string" qi="The message to display." />
          <ap>
              Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              Note: Some actions, such as validation actions, will automatically
              set this variable. If this action is called, it will replace any
              existing message with the new message. It is also recommended to
              set the message back to @EMPTY prior to verification and then set the status only when needed.<br/><br/>
              
                  The manner in which the message is displayed to the user is dependent on the verify client.<br/><br/>
              <e>
                     SetFieldStatusMessage("The value '+@F+' is invalid")<br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>False.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>
              Refer to the top level help of the RuleRunnerLogic action library for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
          </see>
      </method>      
      
      
    <method name="SplitFieldValuePreserveStart" qi="Truncates a fields value starting at a specific string or character, keeping the beginning text in the field.">
      <p name="SplitAt" type="string" />
      <p name="CaseSensitive" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>SplitAt</b> A character or a string of characters where the truncation will start.<br/><br/>

        <b>CaseSensitive: </b> "True" indicates that the comparison should be case sensitive.
        "False" will perform a case insensitive comparison. If the parameter is missing
        or invalid, the parameter will default to "True".<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Splits a Field object's text value at the first instance of the character or string
        specified as a parameter. The result is that the final string is truncated at
        the location where the SplitAt parameter matches the field's text.<br/><br/>

        The action deletes all characters to the end of the string starting from the separating
        character, as well as the separation character.
        All text prior to the separating character remains.<br/><br/>

        The confidence values of the remaining characters is not changed by this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SplitFieldValuePreserveStart("c", "True")</b><br/>
          If the current field object's value is "Description," this action truncates it to "Des".<br/><br/>

          <b>SplitFieldValuePreserveStart("a", "False")</b><br/>
          If the current field object's value is "ABCDEFG," the final result is "".<br/><br/>

          <b>SplitFieldValuePreserveStart("g", "False")</b><br/>
          If the current field object's value is "ABCDEFG," the final result is "ABCDEF".<br/><br/>

          <b>SplitFieldValuePreserveStart("Dog", "False")</b><br/>
          If the current field object's value is "I love cats and doggies." this action truncates it to "I love cats and ".<br/><br/>

          <b>SplitFieldValuePreserveStart("@X.MyVariable", "True")</b><br/>
          This works similar to the other examples however the text for the parameter is obtained
          from the value of the DCO variable called "MyVariable" on the current object
          that is attached to the rule.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the separator character is found. Otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SplitFieldValuePreserveEnd, TruncateFromStart, TruncateFromEnd.<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="SplitFieldValuePreserveEnd" qi="Truncates a fields value starting at a specific string or character, keeping the ending text in the field.">
      <p name="SplitAt" type="string" />
      <p name="CaseSensitive" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>SplitAt</b> A character or a string of characters where the truncation will stop.<br/><br/>
        
        <b>CaseSensitive: </b> "True" indicates that the comparison should be case sensitive.
        "False" will perform a case insensitive comparison. If the parameter is missing
        or invalid, the parameter will default to "True".<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Splits a Field object's text value at the first instance of the character or string
        specified as a parameter. The result is that the final string will have the text removed 
        from the start of the value, up to and including 
        the location where the SplitAt parameter matches the field's text.<br/><br/>

        The action deletes all characters to the end of the string starting from the separating
        character, as well as the separation character.
        All text prior to the separating character remains.<br/><br/>

        The confidence values of the remaining characters is not changed by this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SplitFieldValuePreserveEnd("c", "True")</b><br/>
          If the current field object's value is "Description," this action truncates it to "ription,".<br/><br/>

          <b>SplitFieldValuePreserveEnd("a", "False")</b><br/>
          If the current field object's value is "ABCDEFG," the final result is "BCDEFG".<br/><br/>

          <b>SplitFieldValuePreserveEnd("g", "False")</b><br/>
          If the current field object's value is "ABCDEFG," the final result is "".<br/><br/>

          <b>SplitFieldValuePreserveEnd("Dog", "False")</b><br/>
          If the current field object's value is "I love cats and doggies." this action truncates it to "gies.".<br/><br/>

          <b>SplitFieldValuePreserveEnd("@X.MyVariable", "True")</b><br/>
          This works similar to the other examples however the text for the parameter is obtained
          from the value of the DCO variable called "MyVariable" on the current object
          that is attached to the rule.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the separator character is found. Otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SplitFieldValuePreserveStart, TruncateFromStart, TruncateFromEnd.<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="SplitTextIntoVariables" qi="Breaks a field or variable, using a specified character, into multiple DCO variables with each split segment.">
      <p name="TextToSplit" type="string" />
      <p name="NewVariableName" type="string" />
      <p name="SplitCharacter" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>TextToSplit:</b> Text that will be split into multiple DCO variables based on a specified split character.<br/><br/>

        <b>NewVariableName:</b> The text will be split into new variables in the current DCO object
        using this as the base variable name.<br/><br/>

        <b>SplitCharacter:</b> The character that will be used as the splitting value in the source text.
        This character will be removed from the final split text.

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Splits text into multiple variables based on a split character.  For each separate sequence of text,
        a new variable will be created in the current DCO object, based on the root name provided
        in the parameter "NewVariableName".  Each new variable will be indexed by the suffix "_N", where
        N is an integer starting at 0 up to the number of created variables minus 1.<br/><br/>
        
        The number of created variables is in the same variable with the suffix "_count".<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SplitTextIntoVariables("Here Is;Some Text;To Split", "MySplitVar", ";")</b><br/>
          This example will split the input text into 3 separate variables based on the semicolon
          character.<br/><br/>
          
          These will be the variables created on the current DCO object:<br/>
          MySplitVar_count = 3<br/>
          MySplitVar_0 = "Here Is"<br/>
          MySplitVar_1 = "Some Text"<br/>
          MySplitVar_2 = "To Split"<br/><br/>

          <b>SplitTextIntoVariables("@F", "MySplitVar", ":")</b><br/>
          Assuming this is called on a field that contains the text "12345:678:9ABCD:EFG",
          the field text will be split into 3 separate variables based on the colon
          character.<br/><br/>

          These will be the variables created on the current DCO field object:<br/>
          MySplitVar_count = 4<br/>
          MySplitVar_0 = "12345"<br/>
          MySplitVar_1 = "678"<br/>
          MySplitVar_2 = "9ABCD"<br/>
          MySplitVar_3 = "EFG"<br/><br/>

          <b>SplitTextIntoVariables("@P.MySourceText", "MySplitVar", ":")</b><br/>
          Similar to the previous example, this example will obtain the source
          text from the page level DCO variable called "MySourceText", splitting
          the value of that variable based on the colon
          character.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if there is an error or if one of the parameters is invalid. Returns <b>true</b>
        if the text is successfully split.  If the field is empty, the action still returns <b>true</b>
        and the count of variables created will be 0. No additional variables will be created.
        If the split character is not found in the source string, only one variable will be created
        which will contain the entire source string and the action will still return <b>true.</b><br/>
      </ret>
      <see>        
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="SumFields" qi="Sums children of a DCO object that match the specified name.">
      <p name="TypeOrVariable" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>TypeOrVariable:</b> The type of the field or the name of a variable that will be summed.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        The action locates child fields that match the specified type or child objects that 
        contain variables of the same name as specified in the parameter. 
        If the name refers to a field, then the text value of the field
        will be summed. If the parameter specifies a variable name, then every object that has a variable of that name
        will be summed will have the value of that variable summed.<br/><br/>

        The sum of the values will be stored in a variable on the current DCO node.  The variable name will be
        "SumFields_" followed by the parameter type.  If the parameter provided was "Lineitem", then all of the
        items that matched "Lineitem" would be summed and stored in a variable named "SumFields_Lineitem".
        The result of the sum can then be used by subsequent actions as needed.<br/><br/>
        
        The format of the numbers being added must be in the correct numeric format based on the current locale.
        If there are decimal separators, the number must use the correct decimal separator for the locale.
        If the value is plain text or is not in a number format that is valid for the current
        locale, the value will not be added to the total.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SumFields("Detail")</b><br/>
          This example will look for any fields called "Detail" that are children of the current object
          and look to see if the children contain a variable called Detail.  If the name refers to a type
          for a field, then the text value of the field will be added to the total.  If the name refers
          to a variable of a child object, then the value of that variable will be added to the total sum.<br/><br/>
          
          Assuming the above action was called on a page and the page has 10 fields and 3 of those fields have a variable
          called "Detail", then the values from the variable on each of the 3 fields will be added together. If the value
          of the variable "Detail" is "10" on one field, and the variable "Detail" contains "4.50" on another field and 
          contains "6.25" on another field, they will be added for a total of "20.75". That total will be stored
          on the current page object in a DCO variable called "SumFields_Detail".<br/><br/>                 
          
          rrSet("10", "@X\Field1.AddMe")<br/>
          rrSet("4.50", "@X\Field4.AddMe")<br/>
          rrSet("6.25", "@X\Field7.AddMe")<br/>
          <b>SumFields("AddMe")</b><br/>
          This example further illustrates the above example.  While it is not typical that
          an application would hard code variable values simply to sum them as shown, the intent is to
          illustrate how the SumFields actions works. This example assumes there are at least
          these three child fields on the current object: "Field1", "Field4", "Field7".
          The exact field names could of course be changed to something meaningful for
          the application. This simply shows there are three fields with a variable, although
          there could be more without the variable set. If the variable is not set, then
          the field is simply ignored.<br/><br/>
          
          The SumFields action then looks for all child types called "AddMe" and looks for children
          that have a variable called "AddMe".  Again, the name could be changed to something
          appropriate for the application.  SumFields will add these three values and store
          the result in a variable called "SumFields_AddMe".<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>The parent object that contains the child fields or variables that will be summed.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for information regarding the use of locale in actions.<br/><br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


      <method name="SwitchMMDD" qi="Removes leading or trailing spaces from a field or variable.">
          <p name="Target" type="string" qi="A smart parameter that references a variable or field." />
          <p name="Separator" type="string" qi="The date separator." />
          <ap>
              Smart parameters are supported.
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action operates on a short date that has a format that starts MM/DD or DD/MM and swaps
              the position of the month and day values.<br/><br/>

              The action operates on the string regardless of the date format of the current locale.
              It does not perform any analysis on the date to determine if the two values should be
              swapped, it simply swaps them.<br/><br/>              
              <e>
                  <b>SwitchMMDD("/")</b><br/><br/>

                  In this example, the value "03/09/10" becomes "09/03/10".
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>True,</b> if two separators are found and the values are swapped.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>
      
      
    <method name="TrimSpaces" qi="Removes leading or trailing spaces from a field or variable.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target: </b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to trim.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Deletes extra spaces at the beginning and end of the current Field object's captured text value.
        Spaces that are not at the beginning or end of the field's text value will not be removed.
        If acting on a field text value, the confidence of the remaining characters is not changed by this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>TrimSpaces("@F")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field level object, the current text value
          of the field will be trimmed.  If the value is "456.11_ _ _ _" it will be
          updated to "456.11".<br/><br/>

          <b>TrimSpaces("@X.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          This example will trim the spaces of the value for the
          DCO variable called "MyVariable" on the current DCO object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the parameter target does not resolve to field text or to a DCO variable. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        DeleteSelectedCharacters<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="TruncateFromEnd" qi="Truncates a field or variable to the length specified, removing the text on the end.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="Length" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target: </b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to truncate.<br/><br/>
        
        <b>Length:</b> The maximum length for the value. If the text is longer than this length, it will be shortened to the
        specified length by removing the characters at the end of the text.  If the starting length of the value
        is equal to or less than the specified length, 
        the text will not be changed. The length must be a positive integer.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action adjusts a text string to a maximum size.  If the value is larger than the maximum length,
        the string will be adjusted to the specified maximum length by removing extra characters from
        the end of the string.  If the string is less than or equal to the maximum size, the text
        will not be changed.<br/><br/>

        If acting on a field text value, the confidence of the remaining characters is not changed by this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>TruncateFromEnd("@F","6")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field object, it will truncate the text value
          of the field to a maximum length of 6 by removing extra characters from the end.
          The value "EU0002240" would become "EU0002"<br/><br/>

          <b>TruncateFromEnd("@F","1")</b><br/>
          In this example the value "ABCDEFG" would become "A"<br/><br/>

          <b>TruncateFromEnd("@P.MyVariable","10")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field object, it obtain the value from the
          variable called "MyVariable" that exists on the parent page object. The string
          will be truncated to a maximum length of 10.  If "MyVariable" contains
          the text "123456789012345", it will be truncated to "1234567890".<br/><br/>

          <b>TruncateFromEnd("@P\Field2","10")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field object, 
          this action will adjust the length of the text value
          of a sibling field called "Field2".<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the length parameter is not Numeric or if the target does not resolve to field text or to a DCO variable. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SplitFieldValuePreserveStart, SplitFieldValuePreserveEnd, TruncateFromStart, IsTextLength.<br/><br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="TruncateFromStart" qi="Truncates a field or variable to the length specified, removing the text on at the start.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="Length" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target: </b> A smart parameter that resolves to a target DCO field or DCO variable to truncate.<br/><br/>

        <b>Length:</b> The maximum length for the value. If the text is longer than this length, it will be shortened to the
        specified length by removing the characters at the beginning of the text.  If the starting length of the value
        is equal to or less than the specified length,
        the text will not be changed. The length must be a positive integer.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action adjusts a text string to a maximum size.  If the value is larger than the maximum length,
        the string will be adjusted to the specified maximum length by removing extra characters from
        the start of the string.  If the string is less than or equal to the maximum size, the text
        will not be changed.<br/><br/>

        If acting on a field text value, the confidence of the remaining characters is not changed by this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>TruncateFromStart("@F","6")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field object, it will truncate the text value
          of the field to a maximum length of 6 by removing extra characters from the end.
          The value "EU0002240" would become "002240"<br/><br/>

          <b>TruncateFromStart("@F","1")</b><br/>
          In this example the value "ABCDEFG" would become "G"<br/><br/>

          <b>TruncateFromStart("@P.MyVariable","10")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field object, it obtain the value from the
          variable called "MyVariable" that exists on the parent page object. The string
          will be truncated to a maximum length of 10.  If "MyVariable" contains
          the text "123456789012345", it will be truncated to "6789012345".<br/><br/>

          <b>TruncateFromStart("@P\Field2","10")</b><br/>
          Assuming this action is called on a field object,
          this action will adjust the length of the text value
          of a sibling field called "Field2".<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the length parameter is not Numeric or if the target does not resolve to field text or to a DCO variable. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SplitFieldValuePreserveStart, SplitFieldValuePreserveEnd, TruncateFromEnd, IsTextLength.<br/><br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding smart parameters.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>

  </com>
</rrx>
